vg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;"><path d="M28 10V6h14v14h-4v-7.171L27.414 23.413l-2.828-2.828L35.172 10H28ZM6 28h4v7.172l10.586-10.586 2.828 2.828L12.828 38H20v4H6V28Z"></path></svg></div><p class="FiguresOutlineItem__table__description"><!--[-->Representative commercially and academically available AUV platforms.<!--]--></p></button><a href="#T2" class="FiguresOutlineItem__link" aria-label="View Table 2 in article" data-event="dt_figures_outline-click-view_in_article"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="16px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;"><path d="m28.446 11.85-1.359 1.353a.925.925 0 0 0-.21.3.974.974 0 0 0 0 .735.94.94 0 0 0 .21.3l7.563 7.563H6.96a.962.962 0 0 0-.96.96v1.92a.963.963 0 0 0 .96.96h27.69l-7.563 7.566a.925.925 0 0 0-.21.3.974.974 0 0 0 0 .735.98.98 0 0 0 .21.3l1.359 1.353a.96.96 0 0 0 1.356 0L42 23.997 29.802 11.802a.942.942 0 0 0-.3-.21.982.982 0 0 0-.738 0 .942.942 0 0 0-.3.21l-.018.048Z"></path></svg>View in article </a></li><li class="FiguresOutlineItem"><p class="FiguresOutlineItem__title">Table 3</p><button class="FiguresOutlineItem__table" data-event="dt_figures_outline-click-open_table" aria-label="View {{ title }} in lightbox"><div class="FiguresOutlineItem__table__icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;"><path d="M28 10V6h14v14h-4v-7.171L27.414 23.413l-2.828-2.828L35.172 10H28ZM6 28h4v7.172l10.586-10.586 2.828 2.828L12.828 38H20v4H6V28Z"></path></svg></div><p class="FiguresOutlineItem__table__description"><!--[-->Comparison of single AUV and multi-AUV survey systems.<!--]--></p></button><a href="#T3" class="FiguresOutlineItem__link" aria-label="View Table 3 in article" data-event="dt_figures_outline-click-view_in_article"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="16px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;"><path d="m28.446 11.85-1.359 1.353a.925.925 0 0 0-.21.3.974.974 0 0 0 0 .735.94.94 0 0 0 .21.3l7.563 7.563H6.96a.962.962 0 0 0-.96.96v1.92a.963.963 0 0 0 .96.96h27.69l-7.563 7.566a.925.925 0 0 0-.21.3.974.974 0 0 0 0 .735.98.98 0 0 0 .21.3l1.359 1.353a.96.96 0 0 0 1.356 0L42 23.997 29.802 11.802a.942.942 0 0 0-.3-.21.982.982 0 0 0-.738 0 .942.942 0 0 0-.3.21l-.018.048Z"></path></svg>View in article </a></li><li class="FiguresOutlineItem"><p class="FiguresOutlineItem__title">Table 4</p><button class="FiguresOutlineItem__table" data-event="dt_figures_outline-click-open_table" aria-label="View {{ title }} in lightbox"><div class="FiguresOutlineItem__table__icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;"><path d="M28 10V6h14v14h-4v-7.171L27.414 23.413l-2.828-2.828L35.172 10H28ZM6 28h4v7.172l10.586-10.586 2.828 2.828L12.828 38H20v4H6V28Z"></path></svg></div><p class="FiguresOutlineItem__table__description"><!--[-->Comparison of edge computing and centralized processing.<!--]--></p></button><a href="#T4" class="FiguresOutlineItem__link" aria-label="View Table 4 in article" data-event="dt_figures_outline-click-view_in_article"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="16px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;"><path d="m28.446 11.85-1.359 1.353a.925.925 0 0 0-.21.3.974.974 0 0 0 0 .735.94.94 0 0 0 .21.3l7.563 7.563H6.96a.962.962 0 0 0-.96.96v1.92a.963.963 0 0 0 .96.96h27.69l-7.563 7.566a.925.925 0 0 0-.21.3.974.974 0 0 0 0 .735.98.98 0 0 0 .21.3l1.359 1.353a.96.96 0 0 0 1.356 0L42 23.997 29.802 11.802a.942.942 0 0 0-.3-.21.982.982 0 0 0-.738 0 .942.942 0 0 0-.3.21l-.018.048Z"></path></svg>View in article </a></li><!--]--></ul></div></div></div></aside><!----><!--]--><main class="ArticleDetailsV4__main"><div class="ArticleLayoutHeader"><div class="ArticleLayoutHeader__info"><h2 class="ArticleLayoutHeader__info__title">REVIEW article</h2><p class="ArticleLayoutHeader__info__journalDate"><span>Front. Mar. Sci.</span><span>, 12 November 2026</span></p><p class="ArticleLayoutHeader__info__journalDate"> Sec. Ocean Observation</p><p class="ArticleLayoutHeader__info__doiVolume"><span>Volume 13 - 2026 | </span><a class="ArticleLayoutHeader__info__doi" href="https://doi.org/10.3389/fmars.2026.1973977">https://doi.org/10.3389/fmars.2026.1973977</a></p><!----></div><!----><!----></div><!----><h1 class="ArticleDetailsV4__main__title"><!--[-->IoUT-enabled multi-AUV network architecture with edge intelligence and multi-sensor fusion for autonomous hydrographic surveying in dynamic underwater environments<!--]--></h1><div class="PeopleList"><ul class="PeopleList__list PeopleList__list--hidden PeopleList__list--0"><!--[--><!--]--><!----><li class="last-people"><a class="PeopleListItem" href="https://loop.frontiersin.org/people/3361711" data-event="dt_people_list_item-click-faisal_alsaaq"><div class="Avatar Avatar--size-40 Avatar--text Avatar--grey PeopleListItem__Avatar"><!--[--><span class="notranslate">F</span><span class="notranslate">A</span><!--]--></div><p class="PeopleListItem__name notranslate">Faisal Alsaaq  <!----><sup>*</sup><!----></p></a></li><!----><li class="PeopleList__spacer" aria-hidden="true"></li></ul><div class="AffiliationList"><ul class="AffiliationList__list"><!--[--><li class="AffiliationList__item"><p class="AffiliationList__name notranslate"><!----> Department of Hydrographic Surveying, and Faculty of Maritime Studies, King Abdulaziz University, Jeddah, Saudi Arabia</p></li><!--]--><!----></ul></div></div><div style="display:none;" class="ArticleMetrics"><p class="ArticleMetrics__title"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="16px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;"><path d="M39.798 40.512a1.227 1.227 0 0 0 1.2-1.233v-1.434a1.22 1.22 0 0 0-1.2-1.224H37.02l.024-24.402a1.2 1.2 0 0 0-1.2-1.2h-6.93a1.2 1.2 0 0 0-1.2 1.2v24.45h-3.732V21.54a1.2 1.2 0 0 0-1.2-1.2h-6.98a1.2 1.2 0 0 0-1.2 1.2v15.117h-3.778V8.7a1.2 1.2 0 0 0-1.2-1.2H8.19a1.2 1.2 0 0 0-1.2 1.2v30.615a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1.2 1.2l31.608-.003Zm-21.444-3.9V24.105h1.89V36.6l-1.89.012Zm13.098 0V14.82h1.866v21.792h-1.866Z"></path></svg> Article metrics </p><a class="ArticleMetrics__link" href="#metrics" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_metrics_details">View details</a><!----></div><!----><div class="ArticleDetailsV4__main__content"><div class="ArticleContent"><!--[--><!--]--><div id="h1"><h2>Abstract</h2><!--[--><p>The integration of Internet of underwater things (IoUT) technologies with autonomous underwater vehicles (AUVs) is transforming traditional hydrographic surveying by enabling collaborative, intelligent, and networked underwater observation systems. This review provides a comprehensive analysis of recent advancements in IoUT-enabled Multi-AUV network architectures, edge intelligence, and multi-sensor fusion approaches for autonomous hydrographic surveying in dynamic underwater environments. The review examines the evolution of networked AUV systems, focusing on underwater communication technologies, distributed computing strategies, cooperative control mechanisms, and data fusion techniques that support multi-vehicle operations. Particular attention is given to the application of heterogeneous sensor integration, including acoustic, navigation, and environmental sensing technologies, for improving seabed mapping, marine monitoring, and hydrographic data quality. Furthermore, the role of edge computing and artificial intelligence in enabling real-time decision-making, adaptive mission planning, and efficient resource management within AUV networks is discussed. Key challenges, including limited underwater connectivity, energy constraints, communication reliability, and scalability of multi-AUV systems, are critically evaluated. Emerging research directions, including digital twins, machine-learning-based coordination, and intelligent underwater networks, are highlighted to identify future opportunities for next-generation hydrographic survey applications. This review provides insights into the current state-of-the-art and future development pathways for IoUT-based AUV networks in marine science and exploration.</p><!--]--></div><!----><!--[--><div id="s1"><!--[--><h2>1 Introduction</h2><p>The ocean, which accounts for over 70% of the Earth’s surface, is among the most difficult environments for scientific study, resource utilization, and environmental conservation. Hydrographic surveying, the study of measuring and describing the physical characteristics of oceans and coastal areas, is a key part of this field. Its primary application is the production of nautical charts for safe navigation, but its current applications also include high-resolution mapping of the sea bed, monitoring of coastal dynamics, inspection of offshore infrastructure, and the assessment of marine ecosystems (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B13-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B13a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bartolini et al., 2018<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B57" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Gaur and Prakash, 2024<!--]--></a>). With the increasing global demand for blue economy resources, there is an urgent need for accurate, real-time, and vast underwater data. Hydrographic surveying plays a vital role in maritime safety and environmental protection around the world. The accurate identification of seabed mapping helps to uncover minerals, metals, and coral structures as well as methane seepage at extreme depths (<a href="#B57" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Gaur and Prakash, 2024<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B90" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kim et al., 2010<!--]--></a>). Bathymetric mapping is a key component of coastal management to better understand the sediment transport and volume changes at the local level (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B13-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B13a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bartolini et al., 2018<!--]--></span>). Inspection of offshore infrastructure, such as oil rigs and subsea cables, is critical for preventing environmental disasters (<a href="#B57" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Gaur and Prakash, 2024<!--]--></a>). Additionally, the assessment of marine ecosystems, encompassing monitoring of marine organisms and monitoring of pollutants, is crucial for maintaining biodiversity amidst growing climate change and industrial pressures (<a href="#B112" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lin et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B146" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Omeke et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). Such high resolution and vast spatial extents of continuous data collection are no longer a luxury but a necessity for sustainable ocean governance.</p><p>In terms of technology, hydrographic surveys have progressed from ship-based surveys, which are limited in shallow water and have high operational costs, to single autonomous underwater vehicles (AUVs) (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B13-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B13a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bartolini et al., 2018<!--]--></span>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B44-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B44a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Deng, 2025<!--]--></span>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B42-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B42a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Danson, 2002<!--]--></span>). Single AUVs are mobile and flexible but have limited coverage and are vulnerable to single-point failures (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B35-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B35a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Chen et al., 2023<!--]--></span>). The field is currently shifting from AUVs towards multi-AUV collaborative networks, which enable high-resolution, wide-area coverage by swarm operations (<a href="#B207" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yildiz et al., 2011<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B91" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kim et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). This transition is driven by the Internet of underwater things (IoUT), which focuses on the integration of smart and interconnected underwater objects for 24/7 monitoring (<a href="#B57" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Gaur and Prakash, 2024<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B87" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Khalil et al., 2021<!--]--></a>). These networks also involve artificial intelligence (AI) and edge computing, which allow vehicles to process data on board and make autonomous decisions as they go (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B31-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B31a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Chen et al., 2024<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B177" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Sowmmiya et al., 2024<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B22-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B22a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Budda et al., 2025<!--]--></span>). Traditional systems have very high technical and economic constraints. A major concern is their high operating costs, as traditional ship-based methods demand a large amount of fuel and expertise (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B13-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B13a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bartolini et al., 2018<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B57" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Gaur and Prakash, 2024<!--]--></a>). Although early-generation AUVs are economically viable, they have short battery life and are capable of only brief missions (<a href="#B146" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Omeke et al., 2024<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B96" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kumar and Anand, 2023<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B200" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Xu et al., 2024b<!--]--></a>). Moreover, the detection range of single vehicles would be limited for large-scale operations (<a href="#B80" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jhaveri et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B35-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B35a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Chen et al., 2023<!--]--></span>).</p><p>Conventional hydrographic systems are most affected by environmental restraints. The underwater environment is distinguished by a high rate of signal attenuation and a narrow bandwidth that limits the use of acoustic communication (<a href="#B57" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Gaur and Prakash, 2024<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B5-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B5a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Akyildiz et al., 2005<!--]--></span>). Without global navigation satellite system (GNSS) signals, vehicles must use acoustic positioning or dead reckoning, which can result in large positioning errors, sometimes up to tens of meters (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B13-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B13a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bartolini et al., 2018<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B74" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Hwang et al., 2019<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B44-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B44a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Deng, 2025<!--]--></span>). Stratification effects and asynchronous clocks are dynamic factors that may lead to sensor failures and loss of localization, thereby affecting the data integrity (<a href="#B201" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yan et al., 2020<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B119" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Luo et al., 2022a<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B160" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Scheiber et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B155" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Rahman et al., 2022<!--]--></a>). However, these systems are also often based on post-processing at the surface, resulting in delays in the transmission of data, which means that adaptive mission planning is not possible in real time (<a href="#B57" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Gaur and Prakash, 2024<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B158" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Samson and Nagarajan, 2025<!--]--></a>). These challenges are overcome by the use of multi-AUV networks that rely on distributed sensing and processing in vehicle “flocks” (<a href="#B111" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lin et al., 2020<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B41-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B41a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Danielis et al., 2024<!--]--></span>). A key aspect of this paradigm is the cooperation in mission execution, which includes coordinated path planning and task scheduling for the tracking of pollution or for the adaptive characterization of habitat (<a href="#B112" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lin et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B101" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li and Johnson-Roberson, 2014<!--]--></a>). Software-defined networking (SDN) provides the flexibility of network management to adjust to mission requirements or environmental changes (<a href="#B111" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lin et al., 2020<!--]--></a>, <a href="#B112" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->2023<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B223" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhao et al., 2025a<!--]--></a>).</p><p>This review aims to give a comprehensive analysis of the use of IoUT for multi-AUV networks in autonomous hydrographic surveying. We analyze the role of edge intelligence and multi-sensor fusion for addressing persistent underwater challenges within a broad spectrum of dynamic environments. This work is structured around four pillars, which are interrelated. We first discuss architectures of AUVs with IoUT technology, such as hierarchical architecture and software-defined architecture which support the management of large-scale flocked AUVs and multi-tier edge computing (MEC). Second, communication and sensing technologies are analyzed, with a focus on the 6G subsea wireless communication and fusion of various sensors such as SINS, DVL, USBL, etc., to improve navigation accuracy. Thirdly, multi-sensor fusion and edge intelligence are analyzed, with a focus on the use of filtering algorithms such as Federated Kalman Filters, the application of AI techniques such as offloading, and the real-time perception and autonomy applications. Finally, we identify critical challenges and future research directions, including energy harvesting for sustainable IoUT, secure communication protocols, and robust adaptive sampling methods. By synthesizing these emerging technologies, this review provides a comprehensive roadmap for the development of next-generation autonomous systems. Prior reviews typically address discrete subdomains such as underwater communication, multi-sensor fusion, and digital twins in isolation. By contrast, the present work contributes a unified IoUT-centered framework for multi-AUV hydrographic surveying that integrates these four core pillars. It further delivers a holistic, cross-cutting synthesis of associated challenges and future research directions, a consolidated perspective not covered within any single prior survey.</p><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>1.1 Review methodology</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Literature for this review was identified through structured searches of Scopus, Web of Science, and Google Scholar using combinations of the terms AUV, multi-AUV, Internet of underwater things, hydrographic surveying, edge intelligence, multi-sensor fusion, and digital twin, restricted primarily to peer-reviewed journal articles, conference papers, and review papers published between 2000 and 2026 and supplemented by earlier foundational studies where a technology or platform was first introduced. Records were screened for direct relevance to networked/multi-vehicle underwater operation, IoUT-enabled architectures, or the four pillars defined above; single-AUV studies with no networking, IoUT, or multi-sensor fusion component were excluded unless cited for historical or comparative context. A total of 10 review articles addressing individual sub-themes were identified during this process and are compared against the scope of the present review in <a href="#T1" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><b>Table 1</b><!--]--><!--]--></a>.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><div class="ArticleTable" id="T1"><div class="ArticleTable__header"><p class="ArticleTable__title">Table 1</p><!----><button data-tootip-position="top center" class="ButtonIcon ButtonIcon--black ButtonIcon--medium" aria-label="Expand Table" data-event="dt_articles-open_lightbox-table"><!--[--><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;" color="black"><path d="M28 10V6h14v14h-4v-7.171L27.414 23.413l-2.828-2.828L35.172 10H28ZM6 28h4v7.172l10.586-10.586 2.828 2.828L12.828 38H20v4H6V28Z"></path></svg><!--]--></button></div><div class="ArticleTable__content"><table><!--[--><thead><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Prior review</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Scope of prior review</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">What the present review adds</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></thead><tbody><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left"><!--[--><!--]--><a href="#B138" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Mohsan et al., 2023<!--]--></a><!--[--><!--]--></td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">IoUT architectures, communication, applications, and challenges</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Links IoUT specifically to multi-AUV hydrographic survey workflows and edge/AI processing</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left"><!--[--><!--]--><a href="#B181" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Theocharidis and Kavallieratou, 2025<!--]--></a><!--[--><!--]--></td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Underwater communication technologies</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Integrates communication with sensor fusion, edge intelligence, and digital twins as one pipeline</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left"><!--[--><!--]--><a href="#B208" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yisa et al., 2021<!--]--></a><!--[--><!--]--></td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">IoUT security and privacy challenges</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Positions security within an operational hydrographic-survey architecture rather than as a standalone topic</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left"><!--[--><!--]--><a href="#B128" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Mary et al., 2021<!--]--></a><!--[--><!--]--></td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Underwater IoT trends, challenges, privacy, and security</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Focuses the trends specifically on multi-AUV networking and survey applications</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left"><!--[--><!--]--><a href="#B137" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Mohamed et al., 2026<!--]--></a> (shallow-water IoT)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Shallow-water IoT architectures and challenges</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Extends discussion to deep-water multi-AUV operation and cross-depth communication trade-offs</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left"><!--[--><!--]--><a href="#B74" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Hwang et al., 2019<!--]--></a> (adaptive sampling)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">AUV adaptive sampling methods</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Connects adaptive sampling to edge-AI decision-making and digital-twin-driven mission replanning</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left"><!--[--><!--]--><a href="#B53" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Fernandes et al., 2023<!--]--></a> (cooperative localization)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Cooperative localization for AUVs</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Embeds localization within the broader multi-sensor fusion and network architecture discussion</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left"><!--[--><!--]--><a href="#B97" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lai et al., 2026<!--]--></a> (path planning)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">AUV path-planning models and learning-based methods</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Frames path planning as one function within an integrated IoT-enabled multi-AUV architecture</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left"><!--[--><!--]--><a href="#B151" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Qiao et al., 2026<!--]--></a> (side-scan seabed surveying)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Side-scan sonar methods for seabed surveying</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Situates side-scan sonar as one sensing modality within a multi-sensor fusion and edge-intelligence framework</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left"><!--[--><!--]--><a href="#B123" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Madusanka et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B225" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zocco et al., 2023<!--]--></a> (digital twin)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Digital twin concepts in the maritime/marine domain generally</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Concentrates specifically on the AUV fleet’s contribution to and use of digital twins for hydrographic missions</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left"><!--[--><!--]--><a href="#B222" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhao et al., 2025b<!--]--></a> (bio-inspired swarms)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Bio-inspired swarm robotics for underwater use</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Discusses swarm coordination as part of a wider IoT/edge/AI-enabled network rather than bio-inspired design alone</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tbody><!--]--></table></div><div class="ArticleTable__description"><!--[--><p>Scope of principal prior review papers compared with the present review.</p><!--]--></div><div class="ArticleTable__foot"><!--[--><!--]--></div></div><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--]--></div><div id="s2"><!--[--><h2>2 Fundamentals of IoUT-enabled autonomous underwater vehicle networks</h2><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>2.1 Autonomous underwater vehicles in hydrographic applications</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>The autonomous underwater vehicles (AUVs) have been key to the evolution of the hydrographic surveying field and have provided unprecedented access to complex and dynamic marine environments. This diversity of AUV architectures can enable specialized deployments over a wide range of missions from the smallest bio-inspired units to the largest long-endurance survey systems. Bio-inspired platforms such as the small, low-cost Galatea offer flexible platforms for ambient noise and pollution measurements (<a href="#B167" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Simons et al., 2009<!--]--></a>). The GAVIA AUV is a man-portable system that can be rapidly deployed, especially in logistically constrained environments like under ice surveys (<a href="#B206" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yeo, 2007<!--]--></a>). Survey class vehicles, including the MUN Explorer and Dorado class AUVs, are meant for high-resolution mapping over long distances, to the extent of 100 km for the MUN Explorer (<a href="#f1" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><b>Figure 1</b><!--]--><!--]--></a>) (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B27-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B27a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Caress et al., 2008<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B100" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lewis et al., 2008<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B92" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kirkwood, 2007<!--]--></a>). In the case of ultra-long-range missions, such as the Autosub Long Range (ALR) and long-path dedicated instruments weighing 800 kg, they can conduct missions for months, traversing thousands of kilometers and monitoring the changes at the basin scale (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B20-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B20a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bodenmann et al., 2023<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B69" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Horn et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B15-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B15a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bellingham, 2001<!--]--></span>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B37-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B37a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Collar, 1990<!--]--></span>). These capabilities are extended to the abyss by deep-diving vehicles like the Sentry and Autosub6000 which can explore the deep oceans for geophysical and biological purposes at depths of 6,000 m (<a href="#B132" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Medagoda et al., 2016<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B131" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->McPhail et al., 2009<!--]--></a>). High maneuverability and slow-speed stability are provided by specialized inspection class vehicles such as the Odyssey or resident AUVs for close-proximity work near infrastructure in the subsea environment (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B40-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B40a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Damus et al., 2002<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B83" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kasparavičiūtė et al., 2026<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><div class="ArticleFigure" id="f1"><div class="ArticleFigure__header"><p class="ArticleFigure__title">Figure 1</p><button data-tootip-position="top center" class="ButtonIcon ButtonIcon--black ButtonIcon--medium" aria-label="Download Figure" data-event="dt_articles-download-figure"><!--[--><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;" color="black"><path d="M39.182 24.218 24 39.405 8.808 24.203a.96.96 0 0 1 0-1.358l1.358-1.354a.96.96 0 0 1 1.354 0l10.56 10.56V1.965a.96.96 0 0 1 .96-.96h1.91a.96.96 0 0 1 .96.96v30.1l10.56-10.56a.962.962 0 0 1 1.359 0l1.353 1.354a.96.96 0 0 1 0 1.359Z"></path><path d="M40.32 35.565h1.92a.96.96 0 0 1 .96.96v9.6a.96.96 0 0 1-.96.96H5.76a.96.96 0 0 1-.96-.96v-9.6a.96.96 0 0 1 .96-.96h1.92a.96.96 0 0 1 .96.96v6.72h30.72v-6.72a.96.96 0 0 1 .96-.96Z"></path></svg><!--]--></button><button data-tootip-position="top center" class="ButtonIcon ButtonIcon--black ButtonIcon--medium" aria-label="Expand Figure" data-event="dt_articles-open_lightbox-figure"><!--[--><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;" color="black"><path d="M28 10V6h14v14h-4v-7.171L27.414 23.413l-2.828-2.828L35.172 10H28ZM6 28h4v7.172l10.586-10.586 2.828 2.828L12.828 38H20v4H6V28Z"></path></svg><!--]--></button></div><button class="ArticleFigure__figureButton" aria-label="Open lightbox for Figure 1"><figure class="FrontiersImage"><picture class="FrontiersImage"><!--[--><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=340&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=680&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1020&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 563px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=612&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1224&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1836&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 768px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=654&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1308&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1962&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 1280px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=631&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1262&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1893&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 1441px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=716&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1432&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=2148&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp 3x" media><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp" media><!--]--><img src="https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g001.webp" class="is-inside-mask" alt="Diagram illustrating a multi-layer underwater monitoring system, showing data flow from satellite, ship, and cloud to three autonomous underwater vehicles with sonar, environmental, and navigation sensors, connected through layers for data fusion, edge intelligence, autonomous decision-making, and real-time remote-operated vehicle actions, with ocean depth scale from two hundred to eleven thousand meters on the right." loading="lazy"></picture><figcaption><!--[--><!--[--><p>Generic IoUT-based multi-AUV hydrographic survey architecture, integrating satellite links, a surface gateway, underwater acoustic networking, heterogeneous sensing across multiple AUVs, and ROV support. Sensor observations are fused at the edge to enable autonomous decision-making for adaptive navigation and mission adjustment across platforms.</p><!--]--><!--]--></figcaption></figure></button></div><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>The hydrographic utility of these platforms is defined by their sophisticated sensor payloads and multi-sensor fusion capabilities. Multibeam echosounders (MBES) are still used for most seafloor mapping work, with high-precision bathymetry and backscatter data used for seafloor characterization (<a href="#B94" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kirkwood et al., 2005<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B84" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Keohane and White, 2022<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B36-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B36a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Coffelt et al., 2024<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B226" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zwolak et al., 2020<!--]--></a>). Side-scan sonars (SSS) are often used as a complement to these systems to provide high-resolution imagery of the seabed and obstacle detection (<a href="#B168" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Singh et al., 2004<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B71" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Huang et al., 2018<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B39-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B39a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->D’Spain and Chadwell, 2009<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B219" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang et al., 2025a<!--]--></a>). AUVs can also be equipped with sub-bottom profilers (SBP), which create images of the subsurface to depths of 60 meter below the seafloor (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B27-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B27a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Caress et al., 2008<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B94" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kirkwood et al., 2005<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B26-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B26a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Campbell et al., 2015<!--]--></span>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B37-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B37a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Collar, 1990<!--]--></span>). In addition to acoustics, high-resolution optical cameras and strobes allow ecological assessment and georeferenced visual validation, 3D scene reconstruction, and detailed ecological assessment (<a href="#B196" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Williams et al, 2010<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B20-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B20a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bodenmann et al, 2023<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B195" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Williams et al, 2009<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B165" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Shields et al, 2023<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B140" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Nevoso et al, 2025<!--]--></a>). Other advanced payloads are high-resolution synthetic aperture sonar (SAS) for enhanced imagery (<a href="#B226" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zwolak et al., 2020<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B28-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B28a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Celik et al., 2020<!--]--></span>) and underwater LiDAR for accurate structure inspection (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B43-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B43a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Daramola et al., 2023<!--]--></span>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Mission profiles are customized to the hydrographic task. “Lawnmower” survey patterns are commonly used to achieve a uniform coverage of the study area (<a href="#B46" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Deshmukh et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B184" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Tivey et al., 1997<!--]--></a>). In more complex environments, adaptive sampling and terrain-following modes are required, in which the AUV flight altitude or depth is controlled to maximize sensor performance and safety in rugged terrain (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B27-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B27a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Caress et al., 2008<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B100" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lewis et al., 2008<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B132" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Medagoda et al., 2016<!--]--></a>). Adaptive trajectory design and information-foraging algorithms enable vehicles to focus on regions of high scientific interest or to follow the flow of features such as a hydrothermal plume or the boundary of a meadow of <!--[--><i>Posidonia oceanica</i><!--]--> (<a href="#B84" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Keohane and White, 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B60" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Grimaldi et al., 2025<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B4-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B4a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Ahsan et al., 2010<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B67" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Hollinger et al., 2013<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B59" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Gottlieb, 2014<!--]--></a>). The capabilities enable various applications, such as high-resolution bathymetric mapping (<a href="#B226" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zwolak et al., 2020<!--]--></a>), habitat mapping (<a href="#B195" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Williams et al., 2009<!--]--></a>), and seafloor classification for submarine cable route design (<a href="#B71" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Huang et al., 2018<!--]--></a>). Furthermore, AUVs are essential when inspecting pipelines and cables for industry, in which they employ multi-sensor leak detection systems and automated pipe-tracking to guarantee the integrity of pipelines and cables during subsea operations (<a href="#B140" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Nevoso et al., 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B76" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jacobi and Karimanzira, 2013<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B9-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B9a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Andy et al., 2022<!--]--></span>). Moreover, they are capable of exploring areas that have previously been inaccessible, such as deep-sea vent systems and Arctic isobaths (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B20-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B20a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bodenmann et al., 2023<!--]--></span>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B15-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B15a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bellingham, 2001<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B51" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Dowdeswell et al., 2008<!--]--></a>). To consolidate the platform details discussed above and to address the availability of real-life AUV products for hydrographic surveying, <a href="#T2" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><b>Table 2</b><!--]--><!--]--></a> summarizes the class, depth rating, endurance, and representative application of each named platform.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><div class="ArticleTable" id="T2"><div class="ArticleTable__header"><p class="ArticleTable__title">Table 2</p><!----><button data-tootip-position="top center" class="ButtonIcon ButtonIcon--black ButtonIcon--medium" aria-label="Expand Table" data-event="dt_articles-open_lightbox-table"><!--[--><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;" color="black"><path d="M28 10V6h14v14h-4v-7.171L27.414 23.413l-2.828-2.828L35.172 10H28ZM6 28h4v7.172l10.586-10.586 2.828 2.828L12.828 38H20v4H6V28Z"></path></svg><!--]--></button></div><div class="ArticleTable__content"><table><!--[--><thead><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Platform</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Class/type</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Depth rating</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Endurance/range</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Representative application</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">References</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></thead><tbody><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Galatea</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Bio-inspired, low-cost</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Shallow/coastal</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Short-duration deployments</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Ambient noise and pollution measurement</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B167" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Simons et al., 2009<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">GAVIA AUV</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Man-portable, modular</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Shallow/moderate depth</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Rapid deployment missions</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Under-ice and logistically constrained surveys</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B206" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yeo, 2007<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">MUN Explorer</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Survey class</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Deep-water capable</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Up to ~100 km per mission</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">High-resolution long-distance mapping</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B92" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kirkwood, 2007<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Dorado class</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Survey class</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Deep-water capable</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Multi-hour to multi-day</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">High-resolution bathymetric mapping</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B27-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B27a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Caress et al., 2008<!--]--></span>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Autosub Long Range (ALR)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Ultra-long-range, ~800 kg</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Deep-water capable</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Missions of months; thousands of kilometers</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Basin-scale monitoring</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B20-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B20a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bodenmann et al., 2023<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B69" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Horn et al., 2022<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Sentry</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Deep-diving</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Up to 6,000 m</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Multi-hour survey dives</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Geophysical and biological survey of the deep ocean</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B132" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Medagoda et al., 2016<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Autosub6000</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Deep-diving</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Up to 6,000 m</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Multi-hour survey dives</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Deep-ocean geophysical and biological survey</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B131" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->McPhail et al., 2009<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Odyssey</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Inspection class</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Moderate depth, close-proximity</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Slow-speed, high-maneuverability missions</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Subsea infrastructure inspection</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B40-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B40a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Damus et al., 2002<!--]--></span>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tbody><!--]--></table></div><div class="ArticleTable__description"><!--[--><p>Representative commercially and academically available AUV platforms.</p><!--]--></div><div class="ArticleTable__foot"><!--[--><!--]--></div></div><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>2.2 Availability of real-life AUV/IoUT products for hydrographic surveying</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Multiple systems are commercially produced and actively marketed for hydrographic and survey applications, whereas others remain predominantly academic or institutional research assets. This classification directly influences the practical deployability of IoUT-enabled multi-AUV networks for field operators. Man-portable platforms including the GAVIA AUV are supplied by commercial manufacturers as modular, ready-to-deploy systems, supporting reconfigurable sensor and communication payloads tailored to individual survey campaigns (<a href="#B206" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yeo, 2007<!--]--></a>). Deep-diving and long-range vehicles such as Sentry and Autosub6000 generally function as shared national or institutional research infrastructure rather than assets acquired directly by independent survey operators, a consequence of substantial capital expenditure and demanding specialized maintenance regimes (<a href="#B132" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Medagoda et al., 2016<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B131" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->McPhail et al., 2009<!--]--></a>). Inspection-class systems, exemplified by the Odyssey vehicle, are more accessible via commercial subsea service providers, consistent with their established application within offshore infrastructure inspection workflows (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B40-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B40a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Damus et al., 2002<!--]--></span>). Regarding IoT and Internet of underwater things (IoUT) connectivity, foundational hardware including acoustic modems, IoT gateway buoys, and surface-satellite communication links can be procured from established marine electronics suppliers. Nevertheless, fully integrated, commercially available turnkey multi-AUV IoUT networks matching the architectural specifications have not yet emerged as standard market offerings. Existing field implementations are typically constructed through custom integration of separately sourced AUV hardware, communication modules, and gateway infrastructure for targeted research or survey missions rather than acquired as unified end-to-end systems. This disconnect between the commercial availability of discrete components and mature system-level integration constitutes a tangible practical barrier limiting broader uptake of IoUT-enabled multi-AUV hydrographic survey approaches and represents a notable outstanding research challenge.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>2.3 Internet of underwater things concept</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>IoT refers to the general Internet of things paradigm and its underlying infrastructural principles, as adopted in the title and for comparisons with terrestrial systems, whereas Internet of underwater things (IoUT) specifically refers to the underwater-adapted instantiation of this paradigm. The IoUT represents a specialized subdomain of IoT, extending the IoT conceptual framework into marine environments to facilitate observation and exploration of expansive oceanic domains (<a href="#B154" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Qiu et al., 2020<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B77" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jahanbakht et al., 2021<!--]--></a>). As far as the transmission of data through the air is concerned, terrestrial IoT mostly rely on radio frequency (RF) signals, but the ocean is not a radio-free zone and RF signals are easily absorbed and attenuated by water, as illustrated in <a href="#B138" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Mohsan et al. (2023)<!--]--></a> and <a href="#B137" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Mohamed et al. (2026)<!--]--></a>. Therefore, IoUT adopts a multi-modal communication approach, with acoustic waves being used for long-range communication and optical wireless communication and magnetic induction being used for shorter-range communication at high speeds (<a href="#B138" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Mohsan et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B23-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B23a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bushnaq et al., 2022<!--]--></span>). In contrast to the propagation time of electromagnetic waves in air, which is on the order of a few seconds, propagation time in seawater is on the order of hundreds of seconds, with high latency (<a href="#B85" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Khalil, 2026<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B82" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kabanov and Kramar, 2022<!--]--></a>). Moreover, in underwater networks, the nodes are often deployed in remote and inaccessible areas where battery replacement is not possible, and thus extreme energy efficiency and energy harvesting become key design requirements, which are significantly different from those of terrestrial IoT applications (<a href="#B77" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jahanbakht et al., 2021<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B141" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Nguyen et al., 2021<!--]--></a>). The architecture of an IoUT system for AS is inherently heterogeneous and multi-layered, with both static and mobile components, that enable spatial coverage and data continuity (<a href="#B82" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kabanov and Kramar, 2022<!--]--></a>). The underwater sensor nodes (UWSNs) are deployed at the sensing layer either in the seabed for long periods or at multiple depths to gather physical and chemical parameters (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B23-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B23a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bushnaq et al., 2022<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B85" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Khalil, 2026<!--]--></a>). The nodes are the backbone of the distributed sensing, but their static nature makes surveys less flexible. Mobile AUV nodes, such as autonomous underwater vehicles (AUVs) and remotely operated vehicles (ROVs) are used to connect these static clusters, essentially functioning as data mules and mobile relays that expand the network into dynamic and unmapped regions (<a href="#B154" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Qiu et al., 2020<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B82" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kabanov and Kramar, 2022<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>These robotic agents are critical to mapping the sea bottom at higher resolution and to the inspection of areas where stationary sensors are not present. Effective seafloor-to-end-user communication consists of a strong hierarchy of gateways at the surface and a global communication infrastructure. Surface gateways, which usually consist of moored buoys, special ships, or unmanned surface vehicles (USVs), are the crucial connections between the underwater acoustic environment and terrestrial networks (<a href="#B154" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Qiu et al., 2020<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B77" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jahanbakht et al., 2021<!--]--></a>). These gateways have two types of communication interfaces: acoustic transducers for communication with AUVs and sensor nodes and RF or satellite antennas to relay aggregated data to the cloud. These satellites and cloud resources serve as the backbone infrastructure for large-scale marine data analytics, supporting real-time processing and storage of vast amounts of data and allowing for remote decision-making by human users or through a centralized AI system (<a href="#B77" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jahanbakht et al., 2021<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B85" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Khalil, 2026<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Underwater communication is extremely difficult, especially in this context. In addition to high latency, the bandwidth required for acoustic communication is very small and is generally limited to a few kilohertz, as a result of which a small amount of survey data can be transmitted in real time (<a href="#B138" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Mohsan et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B5-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B5a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Akyildiz et al., 2005<!--]--></span>). Multipath fading is another challenge of the channel, as it is the phenomenon of signal reflection from the surface and seafloor which results in the existence of strong interference and degradation of the signal (<a href="#B137" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Mohamed et al., 2026<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B85" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Khalil, 2026<!--]--></a>). A dynamic environment poses challenges in signal synchronization and link reliability for AUVs due to the Doppler spread caused by the mobility of AUVs and water currents (<a href="#B85" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Khalil, 2026<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B50" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Domingos et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). Such basic constraints will require edge intelligence, along with sophisticated sensor fusion, to process data at source, minimize reliance on limited communication channels, and enable autonomous operation in dynamic underwater environments.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>2.4 Multi-AUV network architecture</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>In dynamic underwater environments, multi-AUV networks for autonomous hydrographic surveying require effective communication management and operational efficiency, which require a robust architecture to address communication challenges. The network architecture defines the swarm coordination (how a signal is spread across the swarm) and multi-sensor information (how information is combined from multiple sensors). Existing works classify multi-AUV network architectures into centralized, decentralized, and hybrid paradigms, which provide different merits for underwater operations with IoUT.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>The software-defined networking (SDN) paradigm is commonly used in centralized architectures to achieve more control and scalability over the network (<a href="#B62" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Han et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B224" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhu et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). These systems consist of a centralized controller, usually placed at a surface station or a leading AUV, which controls the topology and intelligent functions of the network globally (<a href="#B62" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Han et al., 2022<!--]--></a>)—for example, software-defined multi-AUV-based underwater networks (SD-UWNs) employ SDN to improve the traditional architecture for underwater networks, enabling efficient data sharing and early warning obstacle avoidance (<a href="#B62" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Han et al., 2022<!--]--></a>). In addition, deep reinforcement learning algorithms like proximal policy optimization (PPO) are often employed to handle the AUV kinematics and energy transfer, which effectively reduces the average age of information (AoI) and improves data collection efficiency (<a href="#B133" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Melki et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). The single control point approach, however, is not suitable for extreme underwater scenarios where the channels are band-limited, causing communication latency (<a href="#B116" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Luo et al., 2020<!--]--></a>) and unreliable centralized control.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>This paradigm is characterized by decentralized physical actuation and policy inference, often realized through multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) (<a href="#B183" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Tian et al., 2026<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B121" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Ma et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). The major challenge is dealing with the environmental interference and topological changes, and decentralized approaches, such as scene-adaptive embodied intelligence, have been widely developed to achieve good performance under such scenarios, adapting an AUV’s behavior locally without frequent communication with the central coordinator (<a href="#B183" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Tian et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). In the presence of intermittent global communication, AUVs can still track and navigate their targets cooperatively using beacon-based communication models and dynamic switching.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>A hybrid and hierarchical architecture lies between the two extremes—centralized management and local autonomy for decentralized execution can be provided together (<a href="#B224" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhu et al., 2024<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B116" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Luo et al., 2020<!--]--></a>). One of the most well-known architectures is the software-defined MARL (SD-MARL) architecture, which combines the centralized control of SDN with the centralized training and decentralized execution framework (<a href="#B224" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhu et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). The hybrid approach enhances tracking accuracy and communication efficiency by using a central entity to train policies and being executed by individual AUVs based on their local observations (<a href="#B224" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhu et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). Moreover, the multi-modal communication links of the hierarchical frameworks comprise acoustic links, optical fiber links, and blue-green laser links, which are usually managed by a hierarchical structure with gateways, cluster heads, and cluster members (<a href="#B217" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang et al., 2025c<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B91" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kim et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). Such hierarchical systems, including the SCAL-Net architecture, adaptively reallocate the communication resources during operations to enhance the data transmission rate and prolong the mission duration, especially in the case of intensive hydrographic surveying missions (<a href="#B91" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kim et al., 2026<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B142" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Nguyen and Yoon, 2012<!--]--></a>). Hybrid architectures are effective in balancing the compromises between global coordination and local responsiveness in dynamic underwater IoUT environments by nesting autonomy within a larger command structure. A comparison of single AUV and multi-AUV survey systems is summarized in <a href="#T3" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><b>Table 3</b><!--]--><!--]--></a>.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><div class="ArticleTable" id="T3"><div class="ArticleTable__header"><p class="ArticleTable__title">Table 3</p><!----><button data-tootip-position="top center" class="ButtonIcon ButtonIcon--black ButtonIcon--medium" aria-label="Expand Table" data-event="dt_articles-open_lightbox-table"><!--[--><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;" color="black"><path d="M28 10V6h14v14h-4v-7.171L27.414 23.413l-2.828-2.828L35.172 10H28ZM6 28h4v7.172l10.586-10.586 2.828 2.828L12.828 38H20v4H6V28Z"></path></svg><!--]--></button></div><div class="ArticleTable__content"><table><!--[--><thead><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Feature</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Single AUV</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Multi-AUV network</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">References</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></thead><tbody><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Spatial coverage</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Limited to the specific path of one vehicle; time-consuming for large areas.</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Extensive and concurrent coverage of multiple regions; high density</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B215" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B12-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B12a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Banik et al., 2020<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B91" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kim et al., 2026<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Mission efficiency</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Total mission time is high as one unit must cover the entire site</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Significantly reduced mission time via parallel execution</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B12-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B12a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Banik et al., 2020<!--]--></span>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B2-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B2a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Abreu et al., 2015<!--]--></span>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Reliability and redundancy</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Vulnerable to single-point failure; mission terminates if the AUV fails</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">High robustness; other units can compensate for a failing vehicle</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B129" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Matsuda et al., 2012<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Positioning accuracy</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Prone to cumulative sensor drift (IMU/DVL) over long durations</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Enhanced accuracy via cooperative localization and mutual measurements</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B129" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Matsuda et al., 2012<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B120" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Ma et al., 2025<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Positioning method</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Independent dead reckoning, INS, and DVL</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Graph-based, master-slave configurations, and acoustic ranging</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B129" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Matsuda et al., 2012<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B120" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Ma et al., 2025<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Data fusion strategy</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Local sensor fusion for a single environmental perspective</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Multi-sensor fusion across heterogeneous platforms for a unified model</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B215" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B91" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kim et al., 2026<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Comm. complexity</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Minimal; basic link to surface or mother ship</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">High; requires robust inter-vehicle acoustic networking and consensus</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B203" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yan et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B2-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B2a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Abreu et al., 2015<!--]--></span>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Operational cost</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">High per-unit cost for sophisticated sensors; lower fleet management</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Potentially lower per-unit (low-cost AUVs) but higher deployment logistics</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B12-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B12a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Banik et al., 2020<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B64" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Henthorn et al., 2006<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Scalability</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Limited; requires multiple separate deployments for more data</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">High; can be scaled by adding more nodes to the network</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B91" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kim et al., 2026<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">System complexity</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Low; single control system and path planning</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">High; involves formation control and complex collision avoidance</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B203" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yan et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B2-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B2a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Abreu et al., 2015<!--]--></span>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tbody><!--]--></table></div><div class="ArticleTable__description"><!--[--><p>Comparison of single AUV and multi-AUV survey systems.</p><!--]--></div><div class="ArticleTable__foot"><!--[--><!--]--></div></div><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--]--></div><div id="s3"><!--[--><h2>3 Communication technologies for networked AUV systems</h2><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>3.1 Underwater acoustic communication</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Despite the high attenuation of the electromagnetic and optical signals in the aquatic medium, underwater acoustic communication (UAC) is still the most widely used technology for long-distance data transmission in multi-AUV networks (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B32-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B32a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Chen and Pompili, 2014a<!--]--></span>, <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B33-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B33a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->2014<!--]--></span>). However, the physical properties of the underwater acoustic channel pose considerable challenges that must be overcome to establish reliable and effective autonomous hydrographic surveying in dynamic conditions. This makes UAC an ideal option for communication over distances of several kilometers, essential for large-scale maritime operations. Acoustic waves allow long-distance transmission at low frequencies, whereas optical links are only viable for short distances because of scattering and absorption. Despite the high cost of operation in terms of power required to transmit the signal between 10 and 50 W, the long-range capability is very sensitive to the variations of temperature, salinity, and pressure with depth that cause the signal to refract (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B32-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B32a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Chen and Pompili, 2014a<!--]--></span>, <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B33-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B33a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->2014<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B63" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Hazarika and Rahmati, 2025<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>In addition to range limitations, the bandwidth available to UAC is extremely small, typically a few kilohertz in long-range and up to a few hundred kilohertz in short-range applications (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B33-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B33a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Chen and Pompili, 2014b<!--]--></span>). This shortage is mostly caused by frequency-selective ambient noise and a strong rise in absorption with increasing frequency. Therefore, multi-AUV system is required to use advanced modulation methods and adaptive beamforming to achieve maximum information in these narrow spectral windows (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B33-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B33a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Chen and Pompili, 2014b<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B63" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Hazarika and Rahmati, 2025<!--]--></a>). Moreover, the speed of sound in water is about 1,500 m/s or five orders of magnitude lower than the speed of light. This difference creates considerable delays in the propagation path and hence high latency in data propagation. In a networked environment, this latency on its own is compounded by the need for guard times to prevent signal collisions and the need for long packet durations. Due to the high latency, it requires asynchronous communication protocols and smart scheduling to ensure synchronization of the network and freshness of the data (<a href="#B153" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Qiu et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B16-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B16a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bettencourt et al., 2024<!--]--></span>). Underwater dynamics require multiple AUVs to coordinate with one another in order to successfully complete a mission and avoid colliding with each other. In cooperative navigation and formation control, acoustic networks enable the exchange of critical position, velocity, and intent information among vehicles, which is essential for efficient navigation and control (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B16-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B16a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bettencourt et al., 2024<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B72" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Huang et al., 2014<!--]--></a>). Synchronization-based survey (SBS) schemes allow for AUVs to periodically meet up and synchronize their trajectories so that even when one unit experiences a loss of communication, the swarm can continue to conduct a coherent surveying pattern (<a href="#B210" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yoon and Qiao, 2011<!--]--></a>). Spatio-temporal prioritization has improved recently, enabling systems to prioritize coordination signals by urgency, for example immediate detection of obstacles or important state updates (<a href="#B63" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Hazarika and Rahmati, 2025<!--]--></a>). Working efficiently with data is also crucial in the hydrographic surveying area where a lot of data is gathered from a lot of sensors and has to be sent to a surface station. In AUV-aided routing protocols (AURP), AUVs are frequently used as mobile relay nodes to collect data from sensor gateways via short-range, high-bandwidth acoustic transmission and then forward it to a central sink (<a href="#B209" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yoon et al., 2012<!--]--></a>). This allows one to reduce the effect of low bandwidth and high latency by making the communicating nodes physically closer. In addition, bimodal networks, combining long-range acoustic communication with high-speed optical transfer, offer a capable network for the transfer of high-resolution imagery and bathymetric data when in close range (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B16-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B16a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bettencourt et al., 2024<!--]--></span>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>3.2 Optical communication</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>One of the most exciting developments in multi-AUV networks is underwater wireless optical communication (UWOC), as it offers high-speed data transmission that greatly outperforms traditional acoustic communication (<a href="#B104" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li et al., 2025a<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B58" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Gois et al., 2016<!--]--></a>). For autonomous hydrographic survey, the requirement for high-resolution sonar images and bathymetric data to be shared in real time calls for wide bandwidth optical links. The data rate of UWOC systems can reach several megabits per second (Mbps) to more than 10 gigabits per second (Gbps) (<a href="#f2" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><b>Figure 2</b><!--]--><!--]--></a>), which is much higher than the kilobits per second achievable by acoustic communication, depending on the hardware configuration, including blue-green light-emitting diodes (LEDs) or laser diodes (LDs) (<a href="#B104" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li et al., 2025a<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B213" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zayed and Shokair, 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B117" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Luo et al., 2023<!--]--></a>). Large data volumes of raw sensor data need to be shared among all the AUVs to allow for collaborative processing and multi-sensor fusion, which is a key requirement for edge intelligence applications with high-speed capability.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><div class="ArticleFigure" id="f2"><div class="ArticleFigure__header"><p class="ArticleFigure__title">Figure 2</p><button data-tootip-position="top center" class="ButtonIcon ButtonIcon--black ButtonIcon--medium" aria-label="Download Figure" data-event="dt_articles-download-figure"><!--[--><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;" color="black"><path d="M39.182 24.218 24 39.405 8.808 24.203a.96.96 0 0 1 0-1.358l1.358-1.354a.96.96 0 0 1 1.354 0l10.56 10.56V1.965a.96.96 0 0 1 .96-.96h1.91a.96.96 0 0 1 .96.96v30.1l10.56-10.56a.962.962 0 0 1 1.359 0l1.353 1.354a.96.96 0 0 1 0 1.359Z"></path><path d="M40.32 35.565h1.92a.96.96 0 0 1 .96.96v9.6a.96.96 0 0 1-.96.96H5.76a.96.96 0 0 1-.96-.96v-9.6a.96.96 0 0 1 .96-.96h1.92a.96.96 0 0 1 .96.96v6.72h30.72v-6.72a.96.96 0 0 1 .96-.96Z"></path></svg><!--]--></button><button data-tootip-position="top center" class="ButtonIcon ButtonIcon--black ButtonIcon--medium" aria-label="Expand Figure" data-event="dt_articles-open_lightbox-figure"><!--[--><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;" color="black"><path d="M28 10V6h14v14h-4v-7.171L27.414 23.413l-2.828-2.828L35.172 10H28ZM6 28h4v7.172l10.586-10.586 2.828 2.828L12.828 38H20v4H6V28Z"></path></svg><!--]--></button></div><button class="ArticleFigure__figureButton" aria-label="Open lightbox for Figure 2"><figure class="FrontiersImage"><picture class="FrontiersImage"><!--[--><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=340&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=680&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1020&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 563px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=612&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1224&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1836&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 768px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=654&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1308&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1962&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 1280px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=631&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1262&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1893&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 1441px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=716&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1432&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=2148&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp 3x" media><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp" media><!--]--><img src="https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g002.webp" class="is-inside-mask" alt="Diagram illustrating underwater communication methods ordered by data speed and range: optical offers high speed and medium range, RF provides medium speed and short range, acoustic delivers low speed and long range. A surface ship, underwater sensors, and a submersible are depicted, with communication lines showing respective methods." loading="lazy"></picture><figcaption><!--[--><!--[--><p>Underwater communication technologies and their operating range. Schematic comparison of optical, RF, and acoustic links underwater: optical gives high data rates over short to medium ranges in clear water, RF is heavily attenuated and short-range, and acoustic supports AUVs, ROVs, and seafloor systems over much longer ranges at lower rates. Axes show relative data rate and approximate range; not to scale.</p><!--]--><!--]--></figcaption></figure></button></div><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>The effective range of UWOC is very short and typically from a few meters to about 100 meter in optimum conditions (<a href="#B58" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Gois et al., 2016<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B213" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zayed and Shokair, 2025<!--]--></a>). This limitation is due to the high absorption and scattering rate of photons by the water molecules and suspended particles. Therefore, optical links have a critical and strong dependency on clear water, such that the higher the turbidity, the lower the performance of optical links (<a href="#B104" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li et al., 2025a<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B178" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Tabacchiera et al., 2014<!--]--></a>). In a coastal or sediment-rich environment (typical of some hydrographic surveys), the blue-green spectral window is further degraded by the presence of chlorophyll and organic matter, potentially limiting the distance over which reliable communications can be made to less than 10 meter (<a href="#B213" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zayed and Shokair, 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B178" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Tabacchiera et al., 2014<!--]--></a>). Such environmental factors require strong network topologies where AUVs need to have precise spatial proximity to maintain connectivity.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>UWOC in multi-AUV systems revolutionize the way vehicles are coordinated and data shared. Optical beams are extremely directional, which means that a line-of-sight (LOS) link between the moving vehicles needs to be maintained through the use of complex pointing, acquisition, and tracking (PAT) mechanisms (<a href="#B191" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Weng et al., 2023<!--]--></a>, <a href="#B190" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->2022<!--]--></a>). To overcome this, researchers have developed coordination strategies that adopt deep reinforcement learning (DRL) techniques, enabling AUVs to autonomously optimize their beam direction and trajectory to account for underwater conditions and vehicle movement (<a href="#B103" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li et al., 2023a<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B190" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Weng et al., 2022<!--]--></a>). In addition, in order to bridge the gap of short-range sonars, swarms of AUVs are typically used as relay nodes, creating multi-hop networks to amplify the range of fast data transmission over the survey area (<a href="#B104" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li et al., 2025a<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B117" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Luo et al., 2023<!--]--></a>). Such collaborative approaches often involve multimodal communication, with long-range acoustic signals used for coarse positioning for rendezvous and allowing high-speed optical communications for detailed data harvesting (<a href="#B191" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Weng et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B45-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B45a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Deng et al., 2025<!--]--></span>). This synergy ensures that the AUV network can achieve both broad spatial coverage and the high-throughput capabilities required for modern autonomous surveying.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>3.3 RF communication</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Radio frequency (RF) communication is a low-latency, high-speed alternative to the multi-AUV network, but its viability depends on the high attenuation of electromagnetic waves in the underwater medium. The key applications of RF in dynamic underwater conditions are surface-to-underwater-vehicle interactions and special shallow-water applications where multipath or low bandwidth may be a disadvantage for conventional acoustic links. Due to its physical properties of high conductivity and permittivity, the RF signal is rapidly absorbed in water, making underwater-to-underwater communications possible for very short distances, typically centimeters or a few meters. By tuning frequencies in sub-GHz or high-frequency (HF) bands and employing specially designed antenna designs, researchers have successfully demonstrated high-speed data exchange for applications such as proximity-based docking and live video streaming (<a href="#B175" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Soomro, 2017<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B180" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Teixeira et al., 2015<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B6-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B6a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Alahmad et al., 2023<!--]--></span>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>The most typical situation for deployment of RF in AUV operations is surface communication. GPS signals cannot penetrate more than a few millimeters of water, so AUVs have to come up periodically to get a fix on their location and communicate with their internal inertial navigation systems (INS) (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B8-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B8a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Allison, 2020<!--]--></span>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B19-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B19a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bo et al., 2019<!--]--></span>). These surfacing events are the main data upload and mission update connection to RF links. AUVs can communicate with remote operation centers through satellite-based systems such as low earth orbiting (LEO) constellations or directly with surface base stations, unmanned surface vehicles (USVs), or onshore Internet gateways (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B18-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B18a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Blidberg, 1997<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B166" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Shkurti et al., 2012<!--]--></a>). This surface-level connectivity is vital for maintaining the long-term navigational integrity of a multi-AUV swarm, as a single surfaced vehicle can act as a reference node, propagating updated GPS coordinates to submerged peers via acoustic or optical channels (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B8-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B8a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Allison, 2020<!--]--></span>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>RF communication has unique advantages in shallow water applications, especially in the case of crossing the air-water interface. In contrast, the short range of signal transmission in fresh water (5 m) and seawater (1.8 m) makes it possible for high-throughput interactions between AUVs and surface-born sensors and infrastructure (<a href="#B180" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Teixeira et al., 2015<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B1-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B1a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Aboderin, 2019<!--]--></span>). Such short-range links are now being used in hybrid communication architectures using the reliability of long-range acoustic signaling to enable high-speed use of RF for terminal maneuvers. With these systems, a wide range of coordination and low-rate telemetry are performed acoustically, while RF links are used for precision control in docking and/or close-range data harvesting (<a href="#B175" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Soomro, 2017<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B176" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Soomro et al., 2017<!--]--></a>). Previous empirical research has demonstrated that hybrid RF-acoustic docking schemes can achieve up to an improvement of 385% in docking times over acoustic-only schemes and dramatic decreases in steady-state errors (<a href="#B176" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Soomro et al., 2017<!--]--></a>). With the incorporation of RF into a wider network architecture, multi-AUV systems can provide a good balance of long-range networking and high-speed data transmission for the implementation of complex, cooperative hydrographic surveying.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>3.4 Hybrid communication networks</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Multi-AUV systems rely on hybrid communication networks as their underlying infrastructure, which combine the advantages of acoustic, optical, and radio frequency (RF) technologies to create a robust and efficient communication solution (<a href="#B222" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhao et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B30-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B30a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Chang, 2025<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B216" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang and Liu, 2025<!--]--></a>). In complex and dynamic hydrographic surveying environments, the need for coordination over long ranges and the need for high-speed data exchange are in conflict, and no single modality of communication can meet both of these needs. Acoustic communication is indispensable for broad-area coverage as it suffers minimal attenuation over distances of several kilometers but has limitations in terms of low data rates, low speed, high latency, and pronounced multipath effects (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B16-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B16a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bettencourt et al., 2024<!--]--></span>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B10-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B10a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Azar et al., 2022<!--]--></span>). Underwater wireless optical communication (UWOC) provides the rapid communication speed required to transmit high-resolution sonar images and real-time video but is limited to short-range, line-of-sight links and is sensitive to ocean turbidity and blocking of signals (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B16-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B16a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bettencourt et al., 2024<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B118" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Luo et al., 2022b<!--]--></a>). These are complemented by the use of RF technology, which allows high-speed and low-latency control links to various agents that are submerged in water during the surfacing event or very close to the surface, connecting agents to surface-based infrastructure (<a href="#B216" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang and Liu, 2025<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B10-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B10a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Azar et al., 2022<!--]--></span>). Combining these modalities into a single “hybrid” architecture enables multi-AUV networks to maintain the global connectivity using acoustics while opportunistically switching to high bandwidth optical or RF communications in high-data-rate applications or for precision operations such as docking and formation alignment (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B16-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B16a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bettencourt et al., 2024<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B82" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kabanov and Kramar, 2022<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Advanced adaptive communication strategies are used to dynamically change between different technologies as the situation dictates, based on real-time link assessment and mission priority. Artificial intelligence (AI) plays a crucial role in these approaches, with reinforcement learning (RL) and multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) frameworks enabling AUVs to learn the best switching policies by interacting with the stochastic underwater environment (<a href="#B118" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Luo et al., 2022b<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B125" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Mahgoli et al., 2026b<!--]--></a>). An adaptive scheme based on Q-learning can be used to track channel quality indicators, such as signal-to-noise ratio and packet loss rates, allowing the system to automatically switch from an optical link to a stronger acoustic connection when the AUV transitions from a deep-water, clear environment to a coastal, turbid area, thereby avoiding disconnection (<a href="#B118" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Luo et al., 2022b<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B45-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B45a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Deng et al., 2025<!--]--></span>). Moreover, predictive algorithm based on Extended Kalman Filters (EKF) or Extremum Seeking Control can be used to actively control the trajectory and beam direction of the vehicle under high-speed optical link conditions, which is helpful to deal with the mechanical disturbances and localization errors of the autonomous mobile platform (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B16-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B16a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bettencourt et al., 2024<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B118" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Luo et al., 2022b<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>In IoUT-based marine robotics, time-sensitive data need to be transmitted through various heterogeneous nodes with minimal latency, a requirement that these adaptive frameworks must satisfy. Adaptive multi-modal cooperation routing (ALL-M2CR) methods have shown to significantly cut down the end-to-end communication latency by up to 97% from traditional static or rule-based dual-modal systems by combining high-speed air–water links and satellite relays (<a href="#B216" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang and Liu, 2025<!--]--></a>). For cooperative surveying missions, this enables a swarm to stay synchronized and gain collective situational awareness, with AUVs dynamically adjusting their communication method based on their relative distance and local water quality (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B45-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B45a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Deng et al., 2025<!--]--></span>). Multi-AUV networks are able to solve the basic physical limitations of the underwater environment by moving the communication paradigm from single medium to a hierarchical communication architecture with AI assistance, allowing for a high-throughput, low-latency communication network and thereby enabling edge intelligence and real-time multi-sensor fusion for autonomous hydrographic surveying.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--]--></div><div id="s4"><!--[--><h2>4 Multi-sensor fusion for hydrographic surveying</h2><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>4.1 Sensor technologies in AUV-based surveys</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Advanced sensor arrays are essential for the high-resolution hydrographic surveying of dynamic and difficult underwater conditions, and the integration of such systems onto autonomous underwater vehicles (AUVs) is a key requirement. Comprehensive spatial awareness and data fidelity are achieved using a complex suite of acoustic, navigation, and environmental sensors. These instruments can be combined to form georeferenced three-dimensional models of the seafloor and the overlying water column, essential for scientific investigation and industrial use, including infrastructure monitoring.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>The main payloads used for seafloor imaging and topographic mapping are acoustic instruments which transcribe acoustic backscatter into visual and structural information. Modern bathymetry is characterized by the use of the multibeam echo sounder (MBES), which sends out multiple acoustic beams to create the wide-swath coverage desired. Modern AUVs, like the Dorado class, are capable of very-high-frequency systems, e.g., 200 kHz, and can attain the vertical precision of 0.1 to 0.3 m (<a href="#B94" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kirkwood et al., 2005<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B64" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Henthorn et al., 2006<!--]--></a>). These systems can acquire centimeter-scale detail of complex terrain such as mid-ocean ridges and submarine canyons, greatly exceeding surface-towed and deployed systems (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B27-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B27a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Caress et al., 2008<!--]--></span>). Side Scan Sonar (SSS) is also used to create a seafloor texture (also known as photo-like imagery) that has a high resolution. The strength of the return echoes reflected in SSS allows for the determination of the type of substrate (sand, rock, or mud). Dual-frequency chirp systems (e.g., 100/410 kHz) are commonly used for identification of benthic habitats, shipwrecks, and submerged pipelines (<a href="#B182" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Thompson et al., 2015<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B126" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Manik et al., 2017<!--]--></a>). Synthetic Aperture Sonar (SAS) is an exciting development over conventional SSS and is able to synthesize a much larger physical aperture by processing successive acoustic pings, which enables constant across-track resolution over range, allowing the identification of fine-scale features such as gas seeps and hydrothermal vent chimneys (<a href="#B182" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Thompson et al., 2015<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B93" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kirkwood and Caress, 2007<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Accurate surveying of aquatic acoustics is only possible with an adequate navigation package that can offset the loss of GPS signal strength in water. Inertial Navigation System (INS) usually uses high-grade laser ring gyros and accelerometers to output high-frequency information about the attitude, heading, and velocity of the vehicle (<a href="#B94" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kirkwood et al., 2005<!--]--></a>, <a href="#B95" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->2004<!--]--></a>). It is the main reference for keeping vehicles stable and maintaining trajectory accuracy in long-duration autonomous missions (<a href="#B64" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Henthorn et al., 2006<!--]--></a>). The Doppler Velocity Log (DVL) is necessary to bound the inherent drift of the INS by measuring the velocity of the vehicle with respect to the seafloor by measuring the Doppler shift of the acoustic pulse. The combination of DVL and INS information allows for the accurate navigation of AUVs for several hours without any acoustic updates from the surface (<a href="#B64" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Henthorn et al., 2006<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B38-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B38a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Cruz et al., 2017<!--]--></span>). Furthermore, a high-accuracy pressure sensor with a quartz crystal-based sensor array is used to measure the hydrostatic pressure (<a href="#B182" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Thompson et al., 2015<!--]--></a>) to calculate the exact depth of the AUV. To ensure that the measurements are consistent along the tidal bench, they are typically adjusted with <!--[--><i>in situ</i><!--]--> tide data from bottom-mounted pressure recorders (<a href="#B182" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Thompson et al., 2015<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>These environmental sensors give the physical context for a comprehensive understanding of the seafloor data. Standard equipment on AUVs include conductivity, temperature, and depth (CTD) sensors that measure the physical characteristics that define the water mass (<a href="#B126" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Manik et al., 2017<!--]--></a>). These data are important to compute the sound velocity profile (SVP) that is an important input parameter for correcting refraction errors in MBES and SSS data. In addition, sensors for turbidity and water quality measure the turbidity and concentration of chemicals such as dissolved oxygen or water pH (<a href="#B126" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Manik et al., 2017<!--]--></a>). In dynamic systems, such as those with sediment transport, turbidity monitoring is especially important because it may be masking the acoustic signal or may be reflecting geological activity. By effectively combining these various data streams, AUVs can operate with autonomy in the dynamic environments while providing high-fidelity datasets. Using the acoustic imaging and accurate navigation and environmental context, researchers can gain new insights into the underwater environment and geological formations.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>4.2 Distributed sensor fusion techniques</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Distributed sensor fusion plays an important role in multi-AUV networks used for hydrographic surveying, where each agent is able to perform a fusion of data collected by a variety of sensors while keeping the system coherent in the presence of changing underwater conditions. These approaches enable accurate location and mapping in such settings by combining acoustic, inertial, and visual data in decentralized architectures while minimizing the communication constraints of the underwater channel. Kalman filtering is the basic method for AUV navigation and state estimation in real time. Multi-AUV hydrographic surveys are widely using Extended Kalman Filters (EKF) and Unscented Kalman Filters (UKF) to process the non-linear system dynamics and to merge data from DVLs, inertial measurement units (IMU), and forward-looking sonars (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B21-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B21a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bucci et al., 2023<!--]--></span>). AUVs can be equipped with local Kalman filters, in the case of Distributed Kalman Filter (DKF) variants which rely less heavily on a central processing node, and are resilient to communication drops with acoustic communication (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B21-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B21a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bucci et al., 2023<!--]--></span>). They are able to reduce localization drift, one of the main problems in long-duration underwater surveys, by continually updating the position estimate with the fusion of several redundant sensor streams (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B21-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B21a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bucci et al., 2023<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B53" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Fernandes et al., 2023<!--]--></a>) inherent in dynamic underwater environments. These methods are especially useful in the absence of anchor reference (<a href="#B107" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li et al., 2023<!--]--></a>). AUVs can also integrate external data like ocean current maps using belief propagation and message-passing schemes to compensate for the errors that accumulate from the inertial sensor. Moreover, covariance intersection has been commonly used in Bayesian approaches for opportunistic fusion between AUVs when only a short time for communication is available (<a href="#B173" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Song and Mohseni, 2017<!--]--></a>). This method allows for the conservative mixing of information without “double counting” of shared information and without affecting the stability of the filters on the network (<a href="#B53" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Fernandes et al., 2023<!--]--></a>). However, particle filtering (PF) outperforms typical Kalman variants in highly non-linear and non-Gaussian underwater environments. Simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) methods, as applied to hydrographic mapping, employ Rao-Blackwellized Particle Filters to fuse multi-beam sonar data with depth sensors and IMUs for the creation of accurate bathymetric maps (<a href="#B197" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wu, 2024<!--]--></a>). PFs are especially good at detecting and excluding dynamic outliers in sonar images, which can otherwise skew survey outcomes, like marine life or floating debris (<a href="#B197" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wu, 2024<!--]--></a>). Decentralized particle filtering also has the advantage of allowing for cooperative localization using low-cost sensor suites, such as single-beam altimeters and acoustic modems; this enables tracking of state variables such as AUV velocity and local flow perturbations across the AUV team (<a href="#B173" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Song and Mohseni, 2017<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B179" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Tan et al., 2016<!--]--></a>). Lastly, new deep learning fusion architectures are being added to the existing filters to control the huge data flow that a large AUV swarm produces. CNNs and RNNs are used to learn complex features from sonar images and sensor time-series data, respectively, to achieve greater mapping efficiency and accuracy than model-only approaches. These AI-based methods work well in scenarios with model mismatches and time-lagged communication, such as in swarm-based hydrography (<a href="#B120" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Ma et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). With the utilization of graph-based localization and interacting multiple models concept, deep learning architectures enable AUV networks to adapt to sudden environmental changes, including strong currents and acoustic interference, thereby achieving high fidelity for data collection in the most challenging underwater terrain (<a href="#B106" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li et al., 2026<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>4.3 Role of sensor fusion in hydrographic mapping</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Multi-sensor fusion is crucial to the autonomous hydrographic mapping process, linking raw, noisy sensor observations to the production of high-fidelity spatial products (<a href="#f3" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><b>Figure 3</b><!--]--><!--]--></a>) in bathymetric precision. A single sensor-based system can accumulate navigation errors, resulting in inaccurate seafloor maps. AUVs can combine data from DVLs, IMUs, and high-precision pressure sensors to employ powerful SLAM algorithms, including Rao-Blackwellized particle filters, to ensure accurate and stable navigation while enhancing topographic data (<a href="#B197" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wu, 2024<!--]--></a>). For more complex situations that involve acoustic interference obscuring the signals from the DVL, cooperative fusion strategies can be employed to keep positioning errors under 10 meter, resulting in consistent and precise bathymetric grids (<a href="#B120" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Ma et al., 2025<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B29-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B29a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Chang et al., 2024<!--]--></span>). In addition, integration of multibeam echo sounders (MBES) with auxiliary data such as magnetic-beacon observations allows adequate data association needed for mapping features in low topographic relief areas (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B29-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B29a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Chang et al., 2024<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B54" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Fezzani et al., 2019<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><div class="ArticleFigure" id="f3"><div class="ArticleFigure__header"><p class="ArticleFigure__title">Figure 3</p><button data-tootip-position="top center" class="ButtonIcon ButtonIcon--black ButtonIcon--medium" aria-label="Download Figure" data-event="dt_articles-download-figure"><!--[--><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;" color="black"><path d="M39.182 24.218 24 39.405 8.808 24.203a.96.96 0 0 1 0-1.358l1.358-1.354a.96.96 0 0 1 1.354 0l10.56 10.56V1.965a.96.96 0 0 1 .96-.96h1.91a.96.96 0 0 1 .96.96v30.1l10.56-10.56a.962.962 0 0 1 1.359 0l1.353 1.354a.96.96 0 0 1 0 1.359Z"></path><path d="M40.32 35.565h1.92a.96.96 0 0 1 .96.96v9.6a.96.96 0 0 1-.96.96H5.76a.96.96 0 0 1-.96-.96v-9.6a.96.96 0 0 1 .96-.96h1.92a.96.96 0 0 1 .96.96v6.72h30.72v-6.72a.96.96 0 0 1 .96-.96Z"></path></svg><!--]--></button><button data-tootip-position="top center" class="ButtonIcon ButtonIcon--black ButtonIcon--medium" aria-label="Expand Figure" data-event="dt_articles-open_lightbox-figure"><!--[--><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;" color="black"><path d="M28 10V6h14v14h-4v-7.171L27.414 23.413l-2.828-2.828L35.172 10H28ZM6 28h4v7.172l10.586-10.586 2.828 2.828L12.828 38H20v4H6V28Z"></path></svg><!--]--></button></div><button class="ArticleFigure__figureButton" aria-label="Open lightbox for Figure 3"><figure class="FrontiersImage"><picture class="FrontiersImage"><!--[--><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=340&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=680&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1020&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 563px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=612&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1224&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1836&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 768px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=654&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1308&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1962&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 1280px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=631&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1262&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1893&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 1441px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=716&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1432&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=2148&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp 3x" media><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp" media><!--]--><img src="https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g003.webp" class="is-inside-mask" alt="Diagram illustrating a marine internet-of-things data architecture with three layers: the cloud layer (satellite and data center), marine edge/fog layer (research vessel and buoy), and underwater edge layer (three sensor-equipped autonomous underwater vehicles) showing data exchange flows and control commands." loading="lazy"></picture><figcaption><!--[--><!--[--><p>Multi-sensor data fusion framework. The underwater edge layer comprises sensor-equipped AUVs that perform local sensing, multi-sensor fusion, and cooperative intelligence. The marine edge/fog layer consists of surface IoT gateways, including the research vessel and buoy, which aggregate and relay underwater data and distribute control instructions. The cloud layer provides remote data-center resources for global data storage, computationally intensive analytics, and mission-level planning. Solid blue arrows indicate sensed or processed data transmission, whereas dashed orange arrows indicate task commands and control instructions.</p><!--]--><!--]--></figcaption></figure></button></div><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>In addition to bathymetry, sensor fusion is very important for accurate object detection and hazard discrimination. Because underwater environments are often cluttered, the single mode detection can have false positives. This allows AUVs to combine acoustic sonar imagery with optical imagery data to more effectively detect salient objects in different scales and lighting conditions through dual-stream interactive networks (<a href="#B205" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yang et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). This fusion method may be supplemented by the Dempster–Shafer evidence theory, providing more credible decision-making in target search missions, where the presence of marine life or debris may affect mission redirection, resulting in an error (<a href="#B99" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lanyong et al., 2019<!--]--></a>). Hierarchical protocols involve rough acoustic survey in order to allocate limited high-resolution resources only where fused evidence is found to confirm the presence of a target (<a href="#B130" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Matsuda et al., 2017<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Multi-source data integration is also highly useful for seabed classification. Ambiguities in seafloor composition, e.g., fine sand or silt, can only be resolved when acoustic backscatter data are combined with visual textures. AI-enabled fusion systems can integrate these multiple layers and generate high-fidelity classification maps, essential for benthic habitat mapping and resource exploration. The combination of acoustic penetration with optical clarity provides a detailed understanding of the seafloor’s physical and biological properties that would be impossible if either penetration or clarity was used alone.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Last, but not least, sensor fusion plays an important role in environmental monitoring and rapid environment assessment. AUV swarms can collect 3D bathymetry, sub-bottom layers and water column images simultaneously and thus offer a comprehensive picture of changes in habitat or chemical plumes (<a href="#B54" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Fezzani et al., 2019<!--]--></a>). These combined datasets boost the credibility of environmental reports and enable the accurate monitoring of degradation or extension of pollution in sensitive coastal and deep-sea ecosystems (<a href="#B99" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lanyong et al., 2019<!--]--></a>). With AI-powered fusion, the mapping rate stays high even as multi-AUV networks expand while maintaining the accuracy and reliability of the data gathered.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--]--></div><div id="s5"><!--[--><h2>5 Edge intelligence in multi-AUV networks</h2><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>5.1 Need for edge computing underwater</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Edge computing is a key component of multi-AUV networks, which are essential for the current practice of hydrographic surveying due to the special and harsh conditions of the underwater environment. The underwater operations experience physical limitations that make centralized cloud architectures impractical, as contrasted with terrestrial Internet of things (IoT) systems with high-speed wireless networks and constant cloud connectivity. Acoustic waves are the key component of underwater communication with very low bandwidth, high latency, and strong sensitivity to multipath propagation and environmental noise (<a href="#B122" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Ma et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B187" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wang et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). The standard channels are acoustical with data rates that are orders of magnitude lower than those over land, and it is difficult to transmit raw sensor data to the surface stations or to a cloud server during active missions (<a href="#B108" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). Edge computing, which processes and compresses data locally at the sensor source, is a solution to this. In particular, for advanced cooperative bathymetric SLAM (Simultaneous Localization and Mapping) systems, edge nodes are responsible for compressing the large-scale topographical data into compact and low-bandwidth acoustic packets, which enable critical navigation and mapping data to be shared within the limited acoustic channel bandwidth (<a href="#B122" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Ma et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B31-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B31a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Chen et al., 2024<!--]--></span>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>In addition to limited communication, the vast amount of data from contemporary hydrographic sensors creates a processing challenge. Advanced high-resolution sensors that produce vast amounts of raw acoustic imagery, bathymetric point clouds, and water column data in a single survey mission include multibeam echosounders (MBES) and side-scan sonar (SSS) (<a href="#B122" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Ma et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B219" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang et al., 2025a<!--]--></a>). The disparity between the volume of data generated and the capacity to transmit it necessitates sophisticated <!--[--><i>in situ</i><!--]--> processing. Through edge intelligence modules, the AUVs can generate a sonar mosaic and bathymetric reconstruction locally (<a href="#B219" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang et al., 2025a<!--]--></a>). AUVs can process the raw sensor data into relevant metadata and navigational information on the subframe level, thereby alleviating the burden of data storage and transmission for individual agents and the network (<a href="#B219" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang et al., 2025a<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B163" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Shelke et al., 2026<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>This local refinement is also essential for the swarm to operate without having to surface regularly to dump offload data through costly satellites or limited radio communications, covering greater distances with relatively little disruption. Additionally, underwater environments are dynamic, with currents, unexplained obstacles, and transient environmental phenomena, which necessitate AUVs to make fast and autonomous decisions in order to guarantee mission success and vehicle safety (<a href="#B163" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Shelke et al., 2026<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B134" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Men et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). For tasks that require real-time processing, like trajectory planning, navigation avoiding obstacles, and identification of targets, real-time processing is a necessity. Through the effective solution of the high delay and heavy latency caused by the round-trip communication between the AUV and the central controller, the AUV can be used as an intelligent edge node to process information on the spot (<a href="#B134" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Men et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). Edge-deployed machine learning models can classify pollutants, monitor habitats, or detect objects in real time, allowing the swarm to adjust mission parameters accordingly as significant environmental changes are detected (<a href="#B55" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Flores et al., 2021<!--]--></a>). This intelligence decentralization enables the sharing of higher-level information, not data, among the AUVs and the establishment of a common operational picture, called “collaborative swarm intelligence”. Finally, edge intelligence transforms multi-AUV swarms from data gatherers to active and cooperative systems that can maintain long-term autonomy in complex deep-sea scenarios, paving the way for the development of the deep-sea Internet of things (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B31-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B31a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Chen et al., 2024<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B134" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Men et al., 2025<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>5.2 Edge vs. cloud processing</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Edge intelligence in multi-AUV network architectures represents a paradigm shift from cloud-centric processing to decentralized, <!--[--><i>in situ</i><!--]--> computation, a choice governed by the physical constraints of underwater acoustic transmission, namely, low bandwidth, high latency, and high ambient noise, which make the continuous transmission of raw sensor data to remote cloud servers largely infeasible (<a href="#B78" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jahanbakht et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B188" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wang et al., 2020<!--]--></a>). Edge processing addresses this constraint by performing feature extraction, filtering, and prediction at the point of data collection, transmitting only low-volume, high-value results rather than raw telemetry (<a href="#B188" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wang et al., 2020<!--]--></a>), which correspondingly reduces the energy cost of long-distance acoustic transmission relative to cloud computing’s centralized but power-hungry alternative (<a href="#B148" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Pandey et al., 2015<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B7" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Alharbi et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B78" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jahanbakht et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B169" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Singh et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). This trade-off is most consequential for real-time obstacle avoidance and adaptive path planning, where edge computing keeps the control loop close to the AUV and avoids the added latency of routing through landside servers (<a href="#B169" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Singh et al., 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B149" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Periola et al., 2022<!--]--></a>), whereas cloud services remain better suited to long-term data archival and intensive offline model training (<a href="#B157" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Salhaoui et al., 2020<!--]--></a>). <a href="#T4" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><b>Table 4</b><!--]--><!--]--></a> quantifies these differences across latency, bandwidth requirement, energy efficiency, and real-time control.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><div class="ArticleTable" id="T4"><div class="ArticleTable__header"><p class="ArticleTable__title">Table 4</p><!----><button data-tootip-position="top center" class="ButtonIcon ButtonIcon--black ButtonIcon--medium" aria-label="Expand Table" data-event="dt_articles-open_lightbox-table"><!--[--><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;" color="black"><path d="M28 10V6h14v14h-4v-7.171L27.414 23.413l-2.828-2.828L35.172 10H28ZM6 28h4v7.172l10.586-10.586 2.828 2.828L12.828 38H20v4H6V28Z"></path></svg><!--]--></button></div><div class="ArticleTable__content"><table><!--[--><thead><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Parameter</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Edge processing (<!--[--><i>in situ</i><!--]-->/AUV-side)</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Centralized processing (cloud/surface ship)</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">Comparative advantage/detail</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><th valign="top" align="left">References</th><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></thead><tbody><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Latency</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Low: Processing occurs locally at the AUV node or gateway, reducing communication hops</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">High: Significant delays due to slow acoustic propagation (approx. 1,500 m/s) and multi-hop routing</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">~39% reduction in end-to-end latency and 4× speedup in image processing tasks</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B78" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jahanbakht et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B188" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wang et al., 2020<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B169" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Singh et al., 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B149" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Periola et al., 2022<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Bandwidth requirement</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Minimal: Only processed metadata or critical alerts are transmitted over constrained acoustic links</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Extreme: Requires continuous transmission of raw, high-resolution sensor/image data</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Effective trade-off between local computation and narrow-band acoustic transmission</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B78" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jahanbakht et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B188" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wang et al., 2020<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B157" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Salhaoui et al., 2020<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Energy efficiency</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Superior: Minimized radio/acoustic transmission time preserves battery life</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Lower: High energy cost for transmitting large datasets through dense underwater media</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">36% improvement in overall energy consumption; 60% power saving via compressed DNNs</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B78" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jahanbakht et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B148" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Pandey et al., 2015<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B7" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Alharbi et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B169" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Singh et al., 2025<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Real-time control</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Excellent: Enables autonomous obstacle avoidance and adaptive path planning within the local loop</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Limited: Delayed feedback loops may lead to navigation errors in dynamic environments</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Necessary for mission-critical tasks in unpredictable underwater currents</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B169" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Singh et al., 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B149" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Periola et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B157" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Salhaoui et al., 2020<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Network reliability</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">High: Maintains operational autonomy even during intermittent link failures or signal fading</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Low: Entirely dependent on stable, high-quality communication links to the surface/cloud</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Edge intelligence provides fail-safe services for distributed tracking missions</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B188" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wang et al., 2020<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B169" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Singh et al., 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B157" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Salhaoui et al., 2020<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Scalability</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">High: Computational load is distributed across the swarm, preventing central bottlenecks</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Restricted: Central server becomes a bottleneck as the number of AUV nodes increases</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Supports large-scale multi-AUV networks through distributed task orchestration</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B188" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wang et al., 2020<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B7" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Alharbi et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B149" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Periola et al., 2022<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><tr><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Data security</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Enhanced: Local data processing reduces the risk of interception during transmission</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Vulnerable: Massive raw data transfer increases the attack surface for eavesdropping</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">Localized encryption and processing safeguard sensitive hydrographic data</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><td valign="top" align="left">(<a href="#B7" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Alharbi et al., 2023<!--]--></a>)</td><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tr><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></tbody><!--]--></table></div><div class="ArticleTable__description"><!--[--><p>Comparison of edge computing and centralized processing.</p><!--]--></div><div class="ArticleTable__foot"><!--[--><!--]--></div></div><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>5.3 AI applications at the edge</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Edge AI integration enables essential features in multi-AUVs such as autonomous navigation, target identification, adaptive survey planning, and collision avoidance. Under dynamic underwater conditions, these systems can analyze high-dimensional sensor data locally, minimizing communication delay and improving real-time responsiveness in dynamic underwater environments (<a href="#B192" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wibisono et al., 2025a<!--]--></a>). The use of complex cognitive systems and multi-sensor fusion is becoming more and more common in edge-AI-enabled AUV systems for autonomous navigation—for example, the Autonomous Underwater Cognitive System (AUCS) combines SLAM with a Soar-based cognitive architecture to process data from sonar, LiDAR, IMU, and DVL sensors (<a href="#B79" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jayarathne et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). By combining these two, AUVs can distinguish between static and dynamic objects, which enhances the consistency of the map and accuracy of navigation in complex oceanic environments (<a href="#B79" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jayarathne et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). Additionally, deep learning (DL) models, such as convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and recurrent neural networks (RNNs), are used for visual SLAM (vSLAM) and pose estimation with the help of monocular cameras to move in environments affected by traditional positioning degradation (<a href="#B152" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Qin et al., 2022<!--]--></a>). In recent years, the development of vision–language–action models and structured chain-of-thought reasoning has recently made strides toward achieving hydrodynamics-informed navigation control (<a href="#B189" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wang et al., 2026b<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Another key edge application is target recognition, where lightweight algorithms are essential for real-time performance. Recurrent Transfer-Adaptive Learning (RTAL) has been proposed as an image reconstruction method to increase the recognition accuracy of side-scan sonar (SSS) images over multiple AUVs while reducing the interference of environment (<a href="#B211" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yu et al., 2023<!--]--></a>). In addition to acoustic approaches, deep learning models can achieve high accuracy in target localization based on non-acoustic inputs, such as electric fields generated by the movement of the shaft, which have yielded a localization accuracy of 97.8% (<a href="#B199" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Xu et al., 2024a<!--]--></a>). Multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) is also used to realize cooperative target tracking by adopting dynamic-attention and dynamic-resampling switching mechanisms to precisely monitor the multiple targets under ocean current interference (<a href="#B186" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wang et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). The AI techniques enable the autonomous detection, classification, and tracking of underwater objects such as marine life or debris with minimal human input.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>In stochastic environments, adaptive survey planning is crucial for an optimal implementation of hydrographic tasks. Dynamic marine features characterization and task assignment optimization among multiple AUVs use hybrid algorithms based on Task Urgency Biological Neural Networks (TUBNN) and Double Layer Biological Self-organizing Maps (DLBSOM) (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B11-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B11a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bai et al., 2024<!--]--></span>). Reinforcement learning (RL) is commonly used to optimize the efficiency and value of information collection while reducing energy usage (<a href="#B47" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Ding et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>). In software-defined MARL, for instance, libraries, such as MADAC, plan paths with a cooperative obstacle avoidance and task distribution objective (<a href="#B113" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lin et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). Knowledge-graph-based planning can be adapted and optimized in real time by encoding environmental hazards as edge weights (<a href="#B68" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Holmberg et al., 2024<!--]--></a>), which can be helpful in uncertainties.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>One of the basic needs in the safety and coordination of multi-AUV swarms is collision avoidance. The traditional potential field approach has been extended to smoother obstacle avoidance in dynamic environments by using intelligent Adaptive Artificial Potential Field (IA2PF) methods (<a href="#B218" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). In map-less environments, AUVs need to adapt their speed and heading according to the current real-time data received from the sonar to navigate safely around static and dynamic obstacles (which may include non-convex objects) without using a map (<a href="#B199" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Xu et al., 2024a<!--]--></a>), and a wide range of deep reinforcement learning algorithms have been employed for this purpose. The combination of other techniques, such as the Dynamic Window Approach (DWA) with the rapidly exploring random trees (RRT), also allows AUVs to predict the trajectories of moving obstacles, thereby maintaining feasible collision-free paths (<a href="#B189" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wang et al., 2026b<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B68" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Holmberg et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). The combination of these edge AI applications enables multi-AUV networks to perform autonomous hydrographic survey in an unpredictable subsea space with high reliability and efficiency.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--]--></div><div id="s6"><!--[--><h2>6 Artificial intelligence and autonomous decision-making</h2><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>6.1 Machine learning for AUV operations</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Machine learning (ML) is the foundation of the intelligence of autonomous underwater vehicles (AUVs) operating in dynamic environments for hydrographic surveying, which allows a high degree of autonomy in route planning, detection, and classification (<a href="#B97" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lai et al., 2026<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B174" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Song and Siow, 2026<!--]--></a>). In the era of modern multi-AUV networks with IoUT technology, deep reinforcement learning (DRL) is increasingly being employed for efficient route planning and navigation. The information-assisted scheme is a representative DRL scheme that uses real ocean current data and information compression to improve the robustness of path planning.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>6.2 Reinforcement learning for multi-AUV coordination</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Reinforcement learning (RL) and multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) are key techniques for allowing autonomous decision making and efficient coordination in multi-AUV networks in dynamic underwater environments. In contrast to heuristic approaches, RL-based methods enable AUV swarms to adjust to ocean conditions that are not known in advance, like variations in ocean currents, acoustic communication delay, and changing mission requirements. One of the main problems solved by these methods is the dynamic allocation of tasks, where tasks must be prioritized based on both urgency and energy availability of individual vehicles. Various advanced algorithms have been developed to optimize task sequences and data collection delay, including the Automatic Policy Amendment (APAA) and GA-NDQN, which is a hybrid of Genetic Algorithms and N-step Deep Q-Networks (<a href="#B49" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Ding and Zheng, 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B114" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Liu et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). These systems can modify action distributions in real time to optimize the trajectory and enhance convergence performance and mission reliability over those of conventional proximal policy optimization (PPO) or double DQN systems (<a href="#B49" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Ding and Zheng, 2022<!--]--></a>). Moreover, multi-agent Twin Delayed Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (MATD3) architectures have been developed to guarantee the autonomy of decision-making for every AUV when communication between them is intermittent or weak (<a href="#B212" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yu and Zhang, 2025<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>In addition to individual task assignment, RL can be used to enable more advanced swarm behaviors such as formation control, collision avoidance, and cooperative hunting strategies. However, in complex hydrographic surveying, maintaining a rigid but adaptable formation is critical for providing even sensor coverage. However, recent research has shown that end-to-end motion planning with DRL combined with neuro-adaptive backstepping controllers can enable follower AUVs to follow the leader to maintain formation in the presence of obstacles (<a href="#B61" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Hadi et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). In cooperative pursuit–evasion or “hunting” games, multi-step Q-learning (MSQ) and distributional soft actor-critic (DSAC) models have been shown to be effective for getting swarms near 100% success in predicting the motion of the target in active current fields (<a href="#B164" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Shi et al., 2024<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B70" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Hou et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). These models are more effective than traditional artificial potential field models in improving the efficiency of execution and ensuring strong collision avoidance in three-dimensional underwater environments (<a href="#B164" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Shi et al., 2024<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B70" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Hou et al., 2024<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Mission optimization, which involves balancing path planning, mission completion time, and energy usage, is still the ultimate goal of RL integration. Multi-objective optimization frameworks like Multi-Objective Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (MODDPG) are particularly promising in energy-constrained scenarios for minimizing buffer overflows and maximizing data throughput through the network (<a href="#B193" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wibisono et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B47" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Ding et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>). Decentralized MARL strategies involve training with energy-aware reward functions, which enable the swarm to work as a team to maximize the overall mission duration while completing high-priority surveying tasks (<a href="#B193" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wibisono et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>). The focus of current research is increasingly on Meta-RL and simulation to reality transfer techniques that leverage real data from the ocean, such as that provided by NOAA, to enhance these models for their deployment in the real ocean (<a href="#B193" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wibisono et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>6.3 AI-based hydrographic survey optimization</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>In the field of hydrographic surveying, AI optimization is centered on maximizing the value of collected data while minimizing resource use in multi-AUV networks. Adaptive sampling is one of the most important advances, in which AUVs respond to real-time environmental inputs to alter their sensing behavior rather than following a fixed path. Autonomous fleets can use unsupervised learning methods, such as Gaussian Mixture Models (GMMs), to compare their local sensor readings with a global hypothesis, which can then be used to modify the formation to “zoom in” or “zoom out” of areas of high scientific interest, such as thermoclines or chemical plumes (<a href="#B88" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Khoshrou et al., 2016<!--]--></a>). Moreover, Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) is gaining traction for gradient extrema and prediction uncertainty in predicting environmental features in regions where no measurements are available (<a href="#B202" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yan et al., 2019<!--]--></a>). Non-myopic Bayesian Optimization (BO) frameworks of Stochastic Variational Gaussian Process maps have been shown to outperform traditional lawn-mowing patterns in complex and large bathometric missions by taking into account the motion constraints of the vehicle as well as localization uncertainty to prioritize areas with the highest information gain within large-scale underwater terrains (<a href="#B89" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kiessling et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). Another key area of interest for AI optimization is coverage path planning (CPP), which seeks to guarantee full and redundant topographical scanning while maintaining the computational burden of coordinated swarms. To overcome the drawbacks of static piecewise linear functions and alleviate the high computational complexity of the Internet of underwater things (IoUT), improved bio-inspired neural network (IBINN) algorithms have been developed to address dead-zone issues (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B25-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B25a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Cai et al., 2023b<!--]--></span>). The incorporation of search-based pathfinding, including A*, in these neural frameworks enables AUVs to efficiently return to previously discovered or missed areas, ensuring seamless, high-fidelity data acquisition (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B25-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B25a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Cai et al., 2023b<!--]--></span>). Metaheuristic methods such as Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) and Ant Colony Optimization (ACO) enable global observation approaches, suitable for complex and multifaceted marine environments (<a href="#B220" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>). These AI-augmented workflows integrate multibeam echo sounder data with real-time terrain classification and geohazard flagging, resulting in a reported 30% improvement in route efficiency and a significant reduction in the timeline required to convert raw acoustic data into actionable seabed intelligence for offshore field developments (<a href="#B144" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Oluwo, 2026a<!--]--></a>). Lastly, but by no means least, the success of an autonomous hydrographic survey mission is tightly coupled to the energy-aware mission planning since the available energy of the AUV is directly limited by its battery capacity, and oceanic conditions can vary significantly over time. Genetic and evolutionary algorithms are used onboard the agents to perform real-time mission replanning in the case of vehicles facing unexpected energy drains, like strong counter-currents or dramatic changes in water density (<a href="#B161" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Seto, 2011<!--]--></a>, <a href="#B162" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->2012<!--]--></a>). These evolutionary algorithms evaluate the probability of mission failure through continual monitoring of power draw, speed over ground, and internal vehicle dynamics, and enable the AUV to autonomously reduce, increase, and/or move the survey region to ensure maximum coverage within the remaining energy budget (<a href="#B162" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Seto, 2012<!--]--></a>). Adaptive strategies have been shown to deal with a doubling in the size of the survey area or with a sudden drop in energy while maintaining mission viability and integrity of data (<a href="#B161" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Seto, 2011<!--]--></a>). With the ability to anticipate potential energy challenges and adapt the survey path dynamically with deep reinforcement learning and on-board intelligence, AI guarantees the success of multi-AUV networks even in the face of the random environmental pressures associated with dynamic underwater environments.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--]--></div><div id="s7"><!--[--><h2>7 Digital twin approaches for IoUT-based AUV networks</h2><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>7.1 Concept of digital twin in marine applications</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Digital twin (DT) technology application in marine field offers a complex solution to multi-AUV networks with IoUT. A digital twin is a specific virtual representation of a physical object or system, like a marine vessel or an autonomous underwater vehicle (AUV), which can be linked to the real world through a two-way data stream (<a href="#B225" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zocco et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B14-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B14a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Baylon and Santos, 2025<!--]--></span>). Specifically, in underwater scenarios, these digital replicas combine dynamics and environmental data to enhance situational awareness and operational efficiency and lower communication energy consumption (<a href="#B204" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yan et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). Typically, there are three main components: a virtual model of the physical system, real-time monitoring through continuous data streams, and simulation features for predictive analysis and optimization (<a href="#B225" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zocco et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B136" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Menges et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). These systems, such as the EU’s digital twin of the ocean (DTO), create a coherent feedback loop between digital and physical realms to support sustainable marine exploitation and conservation (<a href="#B194" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wickberg and Lidström, 2025<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Virtual representation in marine digital twins can range from basic regression equations to advanced physics-based models, including computational fluid dynamics (CFD) and finite element analysis (FEA) simulations (<a href="#B150" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Provancher et al., 2022<!--]--></a>). Virtual models are developed by integrating vehicle dynamics and environmental parameters such as flow fields to predict conditions based on the states of the collected AUVs for AUV swarms (<a href="#B204" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yan et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). The 3D reconstructed sea test environment and sea vessel trajectory can be displayed using game engines such as Unreal Engine 4 (UE4) in higher-level virtual environments, allowing for a virtual inspection of system performance (<a href="#B105" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li et al., 2023b<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B214" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). They serve as a virtual mirror, capturing the spatial positions and states of the assets in the navigation space and the structural and operational conditions of the assets themselves, including hull monitoring and efficiency analysis of the propulsion system (<a href="#B109" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Liang and Yang, 2024<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B150" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Provancher et al., 2022<!--]--></a>). Digital-twin testbeds are additionally used to orchestrate a “system-of-systems” of AUVs, unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs), and autonomous surface vessels (ASVs) for marine infrastructure inspection (<a href="#B56" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Gadupuri, 2026<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Real-time monitoring is achieved by establishing data links between physical sensors and their corresponding digital sensors (<a href="#B123" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Madusanka et al., 2023<!--]--></a>). In marine applications, these include multi-channel sensor networks comprising LiDAR, SONAR, cameras, and GPS, delivering real-time sensor feedback on parameters including speed, stability, and environmental conditions like temperature and wave dynamics (<a href="#B150" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Provancher et al., 2022<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B105" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li et al., 2023b<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B136" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Menges et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). In underwater acoustic sensor networks (UASNs), a layered digital twin-based network architecture (DTNA) uses local twins for real-time resource allocation and global twins for aggregating data to train collaborative multi-agent reinforcement learning frameworks (<a href="#B172" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Song et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). These real-time streams can be analyzed by anomaly detection systems, which typically involve the use of artificial intelligence and machine learning (e.g., CosLU-VS-LSTM), to alert operators and support maritime safety regulations and ecosystem monitoring (<a href="#B139" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Muthu and Cherubini, 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B109" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Liang and Yang, 2024<!--]--></a>). One such example is the incorporation of <!--[--><i>in situ</i><!--]--> robotic observations with satellite data to monitor harmful algal blooms and oxygen depletion on the SyncED-Ocean platform by <a href="#B147" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Palmer et al. (2025)<!--]--></a>.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>Simulation in the digital twin context allows for scenario testing and forecasting of future actions and reactions in the system as a result of changes in the environment (<a href="#B136" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Menges et al., 2024<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B34-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B34a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Chen et al., 2026<!--]--></span>). The Monte Carlo method is used to evaluate the efficiency of the AUV in bottom object search by simulating AUV motion and the responses of the control system to the sea-state parameters (<a href="#B127" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Martynova et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). Additionally, digital twins enable more complex simulations, such as intelligent docking control based on deep reinforcement learning (DRL) and the generation of “what if” scenarios for fault diagnosis, predictive maintenance, and out-of-distribution detection for autonomous vessels (<a href="#B110" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lin et al., 2026<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B75" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Isaku et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). These simulations can be used for autonomous hydrographic surveying, for mission planning under uncertainty, and for real-time data analysis for mine neutralization or optimization of maritime communication (<a href="#B123" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Madusanka et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B139" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Muthu and Cherubini, 2025<!--]--></a>). The digital twin is a powerful tool that connects the digital and physical worlds, playing a key role in ensuring safe, efficient, and sustainable operations of autonomous systems in dynamic marine environments (<a href="#B136" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Menges et al., 2024<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B66" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Hochberg et al., 2025<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><h3>7.2 Digital twin applications</h3><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>The digital twin (DT) construct in the marine domain is now well established (<a href="#B225" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zocco et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B14-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B14a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Baylon and Santos, 2025<!--]--></span>). Within this architecture, the multi-AUV network constitutes the sensing and actuation layer of the twin: the onboard sensor suite and IoUT connectivity of each vehicle provide the continuous, bidirectional data exchange required to maintain synchronization between the virtual model and the physical fleet, while the twin correspondingly issues optimized mission commands back to the network (<a href="#B225" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zocco et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B14-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B14a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Baylon and Santos, 2025<!--]--></span>). This bidirectional dependency differentiates the multi-AUV underwater case from digital twins of static infrastructure or single-vehicle assets, in which the sensing layer is fixed in location and the update cycle is comparatively slow (<a href="#B204" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yan et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). The IoT/IoUT connectivity is therefore not peripheral to the DT but constitutes its operation.</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>The use of DT technology for IoUT-based multi-AUV networks is a paradigm shift in the planning, execution, and maintenance of autonomous hydrographic surveys. DTs are high-fidelity, mutually informing virtual copies of physical systems and their surroundings, offering an unprecedented degree of control over systems in the opaque ocean environment (<a href="#f4" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><!--[--><b>Figure 4</b><!--]--><!--]--></a>). Across these applications, the AUV fleet functions as the DT’s primary data source and actuation layer rather than a passive subject of representation. Pre-deployment simulation relies on AUV state and trajectory data to validate control schemes prior to deployment (<a href="#B7" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Alharbi et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B115" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lu et al., 2024<!--]--></a>), communication evaluation depends on per-node channel-quality data reported during transit through the water column (<a href="#B124" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Mahgoli et al., 2026a<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B172" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Song et al., 2025<!--]--></a>), environmental and perceptual modelling is populated by AUV-derived sensor streams rather than external instrumentation (<a href="#B204" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yan et al., 2026<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B98" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lambertini et al., 2022<!--]--></a>), and predictive maintenance operates on fleet-level health and battery telemetry obtainable only from the vehicles themselves (<a href="#B115" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lu et al., 2024<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B91" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kim et al., 2026<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B143" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Nie et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). The DT is thus more accurately characterized as a function of the AUV/IoUT network’s data-generating capacity than as a general marine technology in which AUVs constitute one input among several. The main use of DT is in mission testing and pre-deployment simulation to reduce the high risk involved in underwater operations. A DT can be used to simulate complex control schemes like fuzzy logic or proportional-derivative control and validate them in a virtual environment that can simulate real-world physics (<a href="#B7" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Alharbi et al., 2023<!--]--></a>). This 5D digital twin modeling connects the physical world, digital world, twin data center, service applications, and data connections, and it can be used to perform a rapid simulation analysis of how the vehicles respond to different sea states, thereby verifying the robustness of the trajectory tracking and obstacle avoidance behaviors before any vehicle enters the water (<a href="#B115" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lu et al., 2024<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B204" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yan et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). Such pre-deployment verification helps to reduce the virtual–real errors and increase the situation awareness of the swarm, promoting more flexible multi-task scheduling (<a href="#B172" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Song et al., 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B204" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yan et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). In addition to simulation, DTs play a key role in communication evaluation, which is essential due to the stringent limitations of underwater acoustic channels: low bandwidth, high latency. Digital twins facilitate communication-aware autonomy by providing the channel quality assessment with respect to the position and depth of each AUV node (<a href="#B124" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Mahgoli et al., 2026a<!--]--></a>). This allows for the iterative optimization of resource allocation paradigms (RAPD) and the deployment of data-driven state predictors to compensate for inherent acoustic propagation delays (<a href="#B172" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Song et al., 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B185" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Wang et al., 2026a<!--]--></a>). Moreover, semantic communication frameworks embedded in DTs can boost efficiency by sending information relevant to the task instead of raw data, maximizing bandwidth usage within the strict energy constraints (<a href="#B86" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Khalil et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). DT data are used in hierarchical network-based architectures such as SCAL-Net for adaptive reconfiguration of available communication resources for active clusters during the different mission phases, providing seamless connectivity through the mission (<a href="#B91" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kim et al., 2026<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><div class="ArticleFigure" id="f4"><div class="ArticleFigure__header"><p class="ArticleFigure__title">Figure 4</p><button data-tootip-position="top center" class="ButtonIcon ButtonIcon--black ButtonIcon--medium" aria-label="Download Figure" data-event="dt_articles-download-figure"><!--[--><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;" color="black"><path d="M39.182 24.218 24 39.405 8.808 24.203a.96.96 0 0 1 0-1.358l1.358-1.354a.96.96 0 0 1 1.354 0l10.56 10.56V1.965a.96.96 0 0 1 .96-.96h1.91a.96.96 0 0 1 .96.96v30.1l10.56-10.56a.962.962 0 0 1 1.359 0l1.353 1.354a.96.96 0 0 1 0 1.359Z"></path><path d="M40.32 35.565h1.92a.96.96 0 0 1 .96.96v9.6a.96.96 0 0 1-.96.96H5.76a.96.96 0 0 1-.96-.96v-9.6a.96.96 0 0 1 .96-.96h1.92a.96.96 0 0 1 .96.96v6.72h30.72v-6.72a.96.96 0 0 1 .96-.96Z"></path></svg><!--]--></button><button data-tootip-position="top center" class="ButtonIcon ButtonIcon--black ButtonIcon--medium" aria-label="Expand Figure" data-event="dt_articles-open_lightbox-figure"><!--[--><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="currentColor" width="24px" style="vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block;height:auto;" color="black"><path d="M28 10V6h14v14h-4v-7.171L27.414 23.413l-2.828-2.828L35.172 10H28ZM6 28h4v7.172l10.586-10.586 2.828 2.828L12.828 38H20v4H6V28Z"></path></svg><!--]--></button></div><button class="ArticleFigure__figureButton" aria-label="Open lightbox for Figure 4"><figure class="FrontiersImage"><picture class="FrontiersImage"><!--[--><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=340&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=680&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1020&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 563px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=612&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1224&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1836&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 768px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=654&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1308&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1962&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 1280px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=631&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1262&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1893&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 3x" media="(max-width: 1441px)"><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=716&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 1x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=1432&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 2x, https://www.frontiersin.org/api/ipx/w=2148&amp;f=webp&amp;v=7/https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp 3x" media><source srcset="https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp" media><!--]--><img src="https://www.frontiersin.org/files/Articles/1973977/xml-images/fmars-13-1973977-g004.webp" class="is-inside-mask" alt="Diagram illustrating the workflow of an autonomous underwater vehicle (AUV) network, showing bidirectional connections between the physical AUV network, digital twin model, sensor data acquisition, AI simulation, and optimized mission planning within a marine environment, with arrows indicating data flow, state updates, acquisition, processing, and control." loading="lazy"></picture><figcaption><!--[--><!--[--><p>Digital twin framework for AUV hydrographic missions. Closed loop between the physical AUV fleet and its virtual twin: sensor data update the twin, the twin and an AI simulation module evaluate candidate actions, and optimized mission plans return to the fleet, with bidirectional links at each stage.</p><!--]--><!--]--></figcaption></figure></button></div><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><p>DTs improve AUV perception by continuously maintaining a representation of the dynamic environments (floor and flow field) underwater. The twin’s built-in parameter estimators can forecast the local flow conditions using AUV state data, which can facilitate more intelligent navigation and path planning in high-flow conditions (<a href="#B204" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yan et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). The use of digital replicas has proven to be useful for the production of georeferenced survey data for benthic zone mapping, and this can form a basic layer for the wider marine digital twins, as shown in projects like SUSHI DROP (<a href="#B98" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lambertini et al., 2022<!--]--></a>). Furthermore, the structural deformation field can be quickly calculated using model reduction technique, such as proper orthogonal decomposition (POD), which enables the real-time monitoring of the integrity of the AUV platform being subjected to environmental stress (<a href="#B115" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lu et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). Lastly, predictive maintenance with the aid of DTs guarantees the long-term dependability of multi-AUV fleets. With the use of high-fidelity diagnostics, operators can move from a reactive to proactive maintenance schedule with continuous anomaly detection (<a href="#B115" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Lu et al., 2024<!--]--></a>). Digital twins can help monitor battery health and engine performance using collaborative multi-agent reinforcement learning (CMFD) that augments limited real-world fault data with a large amount of simulated twin data (<a href="#B172" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Song et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). To improve the battery lifespan of the swarms, an energy-aware federated meta-transfer learning system can also intelligently re-distribute the tasks to prevent failure of individual nodes (<a href="#B91" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Kim et al., 2026<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B143" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Nie et al., 2026<!--]--></a>).</p><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--><!--]--><!--]--></div><div id="s8"><!--[--><h2>8 Challenges, research gaps, and future research directions</h2><p>Physics of the underwater environment is inherently limiting the deployment of multi-AUV networks for hydrographic surveying, most notably through harsh communication challenges. The underwater communication system is widely different from the terrestrial communication system in terms of the bandwidths of its communication channels, wherein the bandwidth is extremely low and propagation delays are very high (<a href="#B102" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B171" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Sivsakthiselvan et al., 2025a<!--]--></a>). These constraints are further exacerbated by non-Gaussian noise and environmental disturbances, necessitating the development of robust state estimation and information fusion techniques (<a href="#B102" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>). Electromagnetic signals are quickly absorbed by the water and are not reliable, so acoustic or optical sensors must be used, but merging acoustic and optical is still a challenge, especially when the visibility is low and distortions are caused by the water (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B44-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B44a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Deng, 2025<!--]--></span>; <a href="#B65" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Heshmat et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). However, there is a critical research gap as many existing surveys are descriptive, and there is a lack of rigorous quantitative comparisons of performance trade-offs under realistic and dynamic conditions (<a href="#B102" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Li et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>). Moreover, the intermittent underwater acoustic communication also introduces challenges to real-time cooperative formation control and multi-sensor fusion algorithms (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B24-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B24a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Cai et al., 2023a<!--]--></span>).</p><p>Communication difficulties are also tightly coupled with the ubiquitous power limitation problems in AUVs. One of the major constraints is battery usage, where acoustic modems are energy-intensive, and propulsion and high-resolution surveying sensors also consume significant power (<a href="#B171" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Sivsakthiselvan et al, 2025a<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B208" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yisa et al, 2021<!--]--></a>). The traditional routing protocols typically suffer from high energy consumption because of poor cluster head selection and suboptimal path planning, which can result in quick depletion of energy resources (<a href="#B170" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Sivsakthiselvan et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>). There is a pressing need for AI-driven adaptive communications and energy-aware routing strategies that can enhance network survivability while minimizing ecological impact (<a href="#B156" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Rehman et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). Recent studies indicate that energy-efficient multi-AUV coordination for large-scale survey missions is still lacking and that reinforcement learning for optimal selection and data aggregation can enhance the network lifetime (<a href="#B170" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Sivsakthiselvan et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B156" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Rehman et al., 2025<!--]--></a>).</p><p>The coordination in AUV swarms is now autonomous, which adds another layer of complexity, especially in terms of swarms intelligence and decision conflicts. However, the lack of standardized working practices and work procedures for the operations using AUVs, including side scan sonar operations, restricts the efficiency and safety of operations (<a href="#B221" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhao et al., 2023<!--]--></a>). Although intelligent detection of seabed targets and autonomous path planning have achieved certain results, the current method cannot effectively reflect the performance characteristics and motion principles of the heterogeneous vehicles (<a href="#B221" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhao et al., 2023<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B151" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Qiao et al., 2026<!--]--></a>). The limitations in computational power, including the performance of GPUs, also limit the realism of the simulations and speed of the real-time deployment algorithm (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B44-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B44a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Deng, 2025<!--]--></span>). Furthermore, decision conflicts are still an open research problem in decentralized networks, where existing systems lack mechanisms for local exploration and the dynamic nature of the underwater environment (<a href="#B222" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhao et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B48" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Ding et al., 2025a<!--]--></a>).</p><p>As the volume and structure of sonar datasets increase, data management issues are becoming more prevalent. Communication bottlenecks and the limited hardware resources of AUVs to perform computationally intensive tasks hinder the transmission of large datasets between onboard devices and edge nodes (<a href="#B159" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Sánchez et al., 2021<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B208" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yisa et al., 2021<!--]--></a>). To manage the synchronization and fusion of a variety of data from sensors, efficient data management platforms are needed, but existing architectures are not necessarily interoperable for multi-AUV systems (<a href="#B159" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Sánchez et al., 2021<!--]--></a>). Developing lightweight neural network architectures and semantic communication approaches that can compress data volume without compromising the quality of the survey information is still a challenge (<a href="#B65" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Heshmat et al., 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B86" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Khalil et al., 2026<!--]--></a>).</p><p>Lastly, IoUT networks are vulnerable to security and reliability issues. The special characteristics of the underwater environment render the security mechanisms used on land non-viable, rendering UWSNs vulnerable to various attacks, including packet dropping, false data injection, and jamming (<a href="#B208" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Yisa et al., 2021<!--]--></a>). Lack of significant trust in the network or privacy protection methods like federated learning for anomaly detection reduces the confidence and lifespan of the network (<a href="#B170" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Sivsakthiselvan et al., 2025b<!--]--></a>). There are current works on secure marine data acquisition systems and blockchain-based architectures, but there exists a need for an all-encompassing security scheme working in a highly resource-constrained and dynamically changing topology (<span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B3-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B3a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Adam et al., 2024<!--]--></span>; <span role="button" tabindex="0" id="B17-button" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><span id="B17a" class="ArticleReference__scrollTarget" aria-hidden="true"></span><!--]--><!--[-->Bhattacharya et al., 2022<!--]--></span>). Successful transitioning from experimental prototypes to reliable autonomous hydrographic surveying networks in dynamic underwater environments requires addressing these challenges as a multifaceted challenge (<a href="#B81" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Jiang et al., 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B128" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Mary et al., 2021<!--]--></a>).</p><p>A new frontier for autonomous hydrograph surveying exists within the combination of 6G networks, swarm intelligence, and secure quantum protocols. A major hurdle is the creation of the 6G network for underwater communications. This is necessary due to the bandwidth and latency issues created by acoustic communication methods (<a href="#B135" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Meng et al., 2022<!--]--></a>). Next-generation networks have been developed through multi-stage optimization and traffic load balancing to increase packet delivery ratios. Environmental variables (i.e., salinity and temperature) are also accounted for during the optimization process (<a href="#B52" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Engin et al., 2025<!--]--></a>).</p><p>It is worth stating explicitly that a native underwater 6G network is not yet a practical reality: current “6G-enabled” underwater schemes use 6G at the terrestrial or surface-gateway segment of the network (e.g., surface-to-satellite/cloud links) and rely on conventional acoustic, optical, or RF links for the underwater segment itself because of the physical constraints on electromagnetic propagation in seawater. In the near term, 6G is therefore better understood as a driver for the surface and cloud tiers of the IoUT architecture, feeding faster onward connectivity for data that AUVs deliver acoustically or optically rather than as a direct replacement for underwater communication among AUVs.</p><p>AI-driven autonomous survey fleets are moving towards using swarm-based intelligence. Swarming allows multiple agents to share bathymetry data and obstacle locations in real time. This reduces computational complexity when determining coverage path planning (<a href="#B181" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Theocharidis and Kavallieratou, 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B215" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Zhang et al., 2022<!--]--></a>). The volume of data created by the fleet requires decentralized training models. Federated learning (FL) among AUVs provides a solution. FL allows multiple members of a swarm to share model parameters rather than raw sensor data. This preserves energy resources and respects the narrow bandwidth of underwater acoustic channels (<a href="#B135" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Meng et al., 2022<!--]--></a>). Research into asynchronous FL has optimized AUV performance by jointly managing CPU frequency and transmission power to mitigate the straggler effect within highly mobile swarms (<a href="#B198" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Xia et al., 2025<!--]--></a>). In addition, the need for sustainable marine robotics is creating new innovations in energy-efficient routing protocols and bio-inspired sensor networks to extend mission endurance in harsh deep-sea conditions (<a href="#B73" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Hussain et al., 2025<!--]--></a>; <a href="#B181" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Theocharidis and Kavallieratou, 2025<!--]--></a>). Finally, all of the technologies discussed will converge to create digital ocean twins. Digital twins are used to synchronize AUV swarm data with predictive geotechnical and metocean models. Digital twins provide decision-ready frameworks for offshore planning. They also allow for the real-time visualization and management of complex underwater environments (<a href="#B145" class="ArticleReference" data-event="dt_articles-click-open_reference"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[-->Oluwo, 2026b<!--]--></a>).</p><p>A further emerging direction is Physical AI, embodied AI models trained to reason directly about physical dynamics and interaction with the environment. For multi-AUV networks, Physical AI could allow a vehicle to learn generalizable models of hydrodynamic drag, thruster response, and obstacle contact that transfer across missions and sea states, reducing reliance on hand-tuned control loops and mission-specific retraining. Coupled with the DT and edge-intelligence capabilities discussed above, Physical AI represents a plausible future vision for AUV operations and networking, though it remains, at an early, a largely simulation-based stage in the underwater domain and warrants dedicated future research.</p><!--]--></div><div id="s9"><!--[--><h2>9 Conclusions</h2><p>The advancement of IoUT-enabled multi-AUV networks has revolutionized autonomous hydrographic surveying, transforming traditional underwater wireless sensor networks (UWSNs) into the dynamic IoUT. Current progress highlights the critical role of AUV mobility in overcoming the high costs and limited localization accuracy of static underwater deployments. A fundamental takeaway from recent research is the absolute necessity of integrating communication, computing, and control (C3) systems to ensure mission success. Given the extreme bandwidth constraints of acoustic channels, joint optimization of transmission power and computational frequency is essential for minimizing energy consumption while meeting the strict latency requirements of real-time surveying. Central to these capabilities is the integration of multi-sensor fusion and edge intelligence. Advanced algorithms, such as multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) and Deep Q-Learning, allow swarms to autonomously navigate complex terrains, prioritize critical data, and optimize coverage paths. Furthermore, decentralized approaches like federated learning.</p><!--]--></div><!--]--><!--[--><h2 id="h11"> Statements </h2><!--[--><div id="" class="Statement__Authorcontributions"><h3>Author contributions</h3><!--[--><p>FA: Conceptualization, Data curation, Funding acquisition, Project administration, Supervision, Visualization, Writing – original draft, Writing – review &amp; editing.</p><!--]--></div><div id="" class="Statement__Funding"><h3>Funding</h3><!--[--><p>The author(s) declared that financial support was received for this work and/or its publication. This research was funded by the Deanship of Scientific Research (DSR), King Abdulaziz University, Jeddah.</p><!--]--></div><div id="" class="Statement__Conflictofinterest"><h3>Conflict of interest</h3><!--[--><p>The author(s) declared that this work was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest.</p><!--]--></div><div id="" class="Statement__GenerativeAIstatement"><h3>Generative AI statement</h3><!--[--><p>The author(s) declared that generative AI was used in the creation of this manuscript. Grammarly were used to improve the grammar, clarity, and readability of the manuscript. BioRender (<a href="https://www.biorender.com/" target="_blank"><!--[-->https://www.biorender.com/<!--]--></a>) was used for figure preparation. The authors reviewed, edited, and approved the final manuscript.</p><p>Any alternative text (alt text) provided alongside figures in this article has been generated by Frontiers with the support of artificial intelligence and reasonable efforts have been made to ensure accuracy, including review by the authors wherever possible. If you identify any issues, please contact us.</p><!--]--></div><div id="" class="Statement__Publishersnote"><h3>Publisher’s note</h3><!--[--><p>All claims expressed in this article are solely those of the authors and do not necessarily represent those of their affiliated organizations, or those of the publisher, the editors and the reviewers. Any product that may be evaluated in this article, or claim that may be made by its manufacturer, is not guaranteed or endorsed by the publisher.</p><!--]--></div><!--]--><!--]--><!----><!--[--><div id="h12"><h2>References</h2><ul class="References"><!--[--><li class="References__item" id="B1"><div class="References__label"><p>1</p></div><div class="References__content"><p class="notranslate"><!--[--><!--[--><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><span person-group-type="author" class="References__personGroup"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><span class="References__name"><!--[--><!--]--><!--[--><span class="References__surname">Aboderin</span><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></span><!--]--><!--[--><!--]--></span><!--]--> (<!--[--><!--[-->2019<!--]--><!--]-->). <!--[--><i class="References__source">Antenna Design for Underwater Applications</i><!--]-->. Doctoral dissertation. (<!--[--><!--[-->Porto, Portugal<!--]--><!--]-->: 
